__data__.text:
Output transitions were formatted as [precursor m/z] → [product m/z] pairs with associated optimized CE values, indexed by METLIN Identification Number (MID), and made publicly accessible via the METLIN web platform (https://metlin.scripps.edu).'}
{'sample_description': 'The study used visceral adipose tissue (VAT) samples from 10 obese patients who underwent sleeve gastrectomy (SG) and 10 healthy control participants who underwent abdominal surgery for non-metabolic indications; all VAT samples were collected intraoperatively, immediately transferred on dry ice, and under biosafety conditions divided into two portions: one for DIA proteomic analysis (including library preparation and sample analysis) and one for untargeted metabolomic analysis; any remaining tissue was processed and stored at -80 °C; all samples were collected, processed, and stored under standardized protocols to ensure sample integrity, consistency, and availability for future supplementary assays and validation studies.', 'XCMS_configuration_description': 'XCMS package (version not specified, but cited as reference 19) was used for peak detection, retention time correction, peak alignment, and peak area extraction; peak detection was performed using the centWave algorithm with parameters: ppm = 15 and peak width = 5–10 seconds; peak alignment was carried out with a bandwidth (bw) of 5 and a binSize of 0.025; ion features were retained for downstream analysis only if more than 50% of samples in at least one group exhibited nonzero intensity measurements; metabolite identification and annotation integrated HMDB and KEGG databases with accurate mass matching using a mass error threshold of less than 5 ppm.', 'process_procedure_description': "For metabolomic analysis, raw mass spectrometry data were first converted into mzXML format using ProteoWizard MSConvert (version not specified, cited as reference 18); subsequent data processing—including peak detection, retention time correction, peak alignment, and peak area extraction—was performed using the XCMS package; metabolite identification followed the Metabolomics Standards Initiative guidelines; annotation was conducted by integrating information from HMDB and KEGG based on accurate mass matching (<5 ppm); significantly altered metabolites were identified using P < 0.05 and VIP > 1; for proteomic analysis, peptide and protein identification was performed using MaxQuant (v2.6.7) against the UniProt Homo sapiens reference proteome database; quantification used DIA and MS2-based modes, spectral library-based quantification, label-free quantification, enzyme specificity set per digestion protocol, and 'Match Between Runs' enabled; iBAQ was used for protein abundance normalization; FDR was controlled at 1% at both peptide and protein levels; identifications based on single peptides or low-quality spectra were excluded; raw protein intensities were normalized using median centering and batch correction; differential expression analysis used unadjusted P < 0.05 and |log2FC| ≥ 1; functional enrichment analyses (GO and KEGG) were conducted using clusterProfiler R package (version not specified, cited as reference 23); PPI network construction used STRING database (v11.5) with minimum interaction score threshold of 0.4; visualization was performed using Cytoscape (v3.9.1); statistical analyses were conducted in R software (v4.3.2), including paired-sample t-tests or Wilcoxon signed-rank tests, chi-square tests, Pearson correlation, and Benjamini-Hochberg FDR adjustment for multiple comparisons."}
{'sample_description': 'The research utilized both synthetic and experimental mass spectrometry (MS) datasets for benchmarking. For synthetic data, two types of signals were generated: 110,000 single peaks and 110,000 double peaks (with varying signal-to-noise ratios [2–10%], intensity ratios [1–5], and peak resolutions [1.00–2.00]), plus an additional validation set of 13,500 single and 13,500 double peaks with zero noise score; all were inserted into real QTOF human urine raw data at distinct m/z values outside the original mass range to avoid overlap.

All software ran on standardized hardware environments (Python 3.11.7 for MassCube, R 4.3.0 for xcms), and complete processing parameters, scripts, and outputs are archived at https://zenodo.org/records/14159704. Evaluation metrics included feature detection accuracy (vs. manual labels), computational speed, and robustness to peak shape variability (e.g., doublet resolution, noise, asymmetry).'}
{'sample_description': 'Articular cartilage samples were harvested from ten Kashin-Beck disease (KBD) patients and ten osteoarthritis (OA) patients undergoing primary total knee arthroplasty. All samples were obtained from the lateral tibial plateau within one hour post-surgery, immediately transported to the laboratory on ice, cut into 1 mm pieces, divided into three portions per sample, flash-frozen in liquid nitrogen overnight, and stored at -80°C. For metabolite extraction, 100 mg of thawed cartilage was homogenized with 120 μL of precooled 50% methanol buffer, vortexed, incubated at room temperature for 10 minutes, then stored overnight at -20°C. After centrifugation at 4,000× g for 20 minutes, supernatants were transferred to 96-well plates; pooled quality control (QC) samples were prepared by combining 10 μL from each extraction. All samples were stored at -80°C prior to LC-MS analysis.', 'XCMS_configuration_description': 'XCMS software (Siuzdak Lab, USA) was used for LC-MS raw data preprocessing, including peak picking, peak grouping, retention time (RT) correction, second peak grouping, and annotation of isotopes and adducts. Raw data files were first converted to mzXML format. XCMS performed nonlinear RT alignment, matched filtration, peak detection, and peak matching. CAMERA (a Bioconductor package) was integrated for compound spectrum extraction, isotope/adduct annotation, and accurate mass proposal. metaX (an R package) was used for downstream processing: features detected in <50% of QC samples or <80% of biological samples were removed; missing values were imputed using the k-nearest neighbor algorithm; quality control-based robust locally estimated scatterplot smoothing (LOESS) signal correction was applied to correct injection-order-dependent signal drift; and relative standard deviations (RSD) >30% across QC samples were filtered out.', 'process_procedure_description': 'The untargeted metabolomic workflow included: (1) Sample preparation as described above; (2) LC-MS analysis using a Thermo Scientific UltiMate 3000 HPLC coupled to a Q-Exactive high-resolution tandem mass spectrometer; chromatographic separation employed an ACQUITY UPLC BEH C18 column (100 mm × 2.1 mm, 1.8 µm) at 35°C, flow rate 0.4 mL/min, mobile phases A (water + 0.1% formic acid) and B (acetonitrile + 0.1% formic acid), with gradient elution: 0–0.5 min 5% B, 0.5–7 min 5–100% B, 7–8 min 100% B, 8–8.1 min 100–5% B, 8.1–10 min 5% B; injection volume 4 µL; (3) Mass spectrometry operated in both positive and negative ion modes: precursor spectra (m/z 70–1050) acquired at 70,000 resolution (AGC target 3e6, max injection time 100 ms); data-dependent acquisition (DDA) with top-three selection; fragment spectra at 17,500 resolution (AGC target 1e5, max injection time 80 ms); QC samples injected after every 10 biological samples; (4) Metabolite annotation via KEGG database matching (mass tolerance <10 ppm) and validation using isotopic distribution measurements and an in-house fragment spectrum library; (5) Statistical analysis: independent-samples t-tests for group comparisons; PLS-DA via metaX for supervised discrimination; VIP ≥1.0, |fold change| ≥1.2 or ≤0.83, and p<0.05 used to define differential metabolites; PCA for outlier and batch effect assessment.'}
{'sample_description': 'Three pools of cortical and trabecular bone powders were obtained from bone samples of 10 elderly patients (males and females) aged 65–85 years who underwent elective hip replacement surgery at IRCCS Istituto Ortopedico Galeazzi, Milan, Italy.

Heat maps enabled comparative visualization of metabolite profiles across UM1/siAK2, UM2/siAK2, UM1/siPGK1, and UM2/siPGK1 conditions.'}
{'sample_description': 'A sample of S. schimperi seeds was collected from Menz Mama Midir district, North Shewa Zone, Ethiopia between January 15 and February 14, 2022; identified and recorded as voucher number MA001 at the National Herbarium, Addis Ababa University, College of Natural and Computational Sciences; the voucher specimen was deposited at the same herbarium; samples were manually cleaned, dried, packed in hermetic plastic vessels, and stored at 5°C until analysis; roasting was performed at 185°C for 15 minutes using an electrical drying oven to simulate traditional community usage; both raw and roasted seed samples were used for comparative untargeted metabolomics analysis.', 'MZMine_configuration_description': 'MZmine software (v3.7.0, Windows version) was used to preprocess raw LC-MS spectra data; raw data was imported and processed for mass detection (centroid), chromatogram building, deisotoping, peak alignment, and filling missing peak data; noise level parameters were set to 2.0E3 for MS1 and 3.0E2 for MS2; the ADAP chromatogram builder was executed with scan-to-scan mass accuracy set at 0.005 Da or 10 ppm; all other parameters were kept at default values; the resulting MS1 feature data was exported and saved as an Excel (.csv) file, while MS2 feature data was exported and saved as an MGF file in SIRIUS/CSI:FingerID format.', 'process_procedure_description': "The metabolomic workflow involved multiple software tools: (1) MZmine v3.7.0 was used for initial preprocessing—importing raw LC-MS data, centroid mass detection, chromatogram building (ADAP), deisotoping, peak alignment, and missing peak filling with noise thresholds 2.0E3 (MS1) and 3.0E2 (MS2), and export of MS1 features (.csv) and MS2 features (MGF); (2) SIRIUS v5.0 imported the MGF file for MS2-based compound annotation; molecular formula prediction was enabled with ZODIAC, CSI:FingerID, and CANOPUS activated; the 'compute all' tab was selected to run molecular formula prediction and structure elucidation; compounds were ranked by Sirius score, and only those with COSMIC confidence score > 0.1, ZODIAC score ≥ 50%, and Sirius score ≥ 50% (or supported by ZODIAC) were retained; top candidates were searched against Natural Products, COCONUT, and CHEBI databases; (3) XCMS Online (https://xcmsonline.scripps.edu/) and XCMS R package (v3.20.0) were used for parallel feature extraction and alignment—XCMS Online employed 'pairwise' job creation and 'HPLC/single quad' parameter settings, while XCMS R used command-line processing; both enabled METLIN-based compound annotation; (4) BindingDB and Therapeutic Target Database (TTD) were used for biological target prediction by importing canonical SMILES of SIRIUS-validated compounds; BindingDB filtering required ≥85% similarity to known ligands; (5) MetaboAnalyst 5.0 performed statistical analysis on XCMS Online output CSV files—data were normalized by sum and auto-scaled, followed by PCA (5 components) and OPLS-DA; signature metabolites were selected based on p-value < 0.05, FDR-corrected q-value < 0.05, fold change > 2.0, and VIP > 1.2; model validation included tenfold cross-validation (reporting accuracy, Q², R²) and permutation testing (p < 0.01)."}
{'sample_description': 'The sample used in the research was the ethanol extract of dried leaves of Uncaria nervosa Elmer (1.932 g), collected from the Kampar Forest, Riau, Indonesia, and authenticated at the ANDA Herbarium, Andalas University, Padang, with voucher number NR-04. The crude ethanol extract (261.75 g, 26.6% yield) was subjected to liquid-liquid fractionation using n-hexane, ethyl acetate, and butanol, yielding 10.15 g, 31.34 g, and 19.21 g, respectively; the ethyl acetate fraction (31.34 g) was selected for further metabolite profiling and isolation.

Calibration was assessed via calibration curves and model-agnostic calibration error metrics; clinical utility was evaluated using decision curve analysis (DCA). The optimal model was deployed as a web application using the Streamlit Python framework, incorporating SHAP force plots and LIME explanations for transparent, real-time predictions.'}
{'sample_description': 'The sample consists of spatial proteomics data with observations X = (X1, ..., XN), where each observation Xn = [Xn,1, ..., Xn,P]⊤ represents a protein measurement vector of dimension P (e.g., protein abundance profiles across cellular compartments); marker proteins with known labels are included and explicitly excluded from outlier consideration; the data may originate from multiple datasets integrated via the MDI framework, and includes both labeled (e.g., compartment-specific marker proteins) and unlabeled proteins for unsupervised or semi-supervised modeling.', 'kNN_configuration_description': 'The kNN transfer learning algorithm is described as a supervised method for multi-modal data in spatial proteomics, but the provided text does not specify any configuration details—including the value of k, distance metric, weighting scheme, neighbor selection strategy, implementation library, hyperparameters, preprocessing steps, or integration protocol with other models (e.g., TAGM or GP mixture). No parameters, settings, software name, or computational details related to kNN are given in the materials and methods section.', 'process_procedure_description': 'The research employs three primary statistical modeling frameworks: (1) Gaussian Mixture Models (GMMs) as foundational probabilistic models, extended to handle outliers via the t-augmented Gaussian mixture (TAGM) model—where each component combines a multivariate normal (MVN) density and a shared multivariate t-distribution (MVT) with fixed parameters (η = 4, M = empirical mean, V = half the global variance), binary outlier indicators ψn ~ Bernoulli(1−ϵ), component allocations cn ~ Categorical(π), π ~ Dirichlet(α/K, ..., α/K), and component parameters (μk, Σk) ~ Normal-Inverse-Wishart(μ0, κ, ν, Ψ); (2) Gaussian Process (GP) mixture models, which place a GP prior over component mean vectors μk, using a zero-mean function and squared exponential covariance kernel c(t,t′) = a² exp{−d(t,t′)²/(2l²)} with Euclidean distance d(·,·) and hyperparameters amplitude a and length-scale l, and deriving posterior means and covariances via standard GP regression formulas involving kernel matrices C(X,X), C(X,X*), and noise variance σ²; and (3) Multiple Dataset Integration (MDI), referenced as model [40], though no specific procedure, alignment strategy, normalization method, or integration algorithm is detailed. Empirical priors from Fraley & Raftery [43] are used for GMMs; for categorical components, priors are set to average class proportions per feature. All models are hierarchical Bayesian, supporting uncertainty propagation, and inference is implied to be performed via standard Bayesian techniques (e.g., variational inference or MCMC), though exact inference methodology is not specified.'}
{'sample_description': 'The sample used in the research consists of surface electromyography (sEMG) signals acquired from a wearable 8-channel dry-electrode EMG armband placed circumferentially around the forearm, with targeted selection of two channels aligned anatomically over the biceps brachii (for elbow flexion) and triceps brachii (for elbow extension); signals were recorded during repeated, labeled trials of voluntary elbow flexion and extension movements performed by human participants; EMG data were sampled at 200 Hz, preprocessed with a 4th-order Butterworth band-pass filter (20–150 Hz), segmented into 250 ms windows with 50% overlap, and annotated for fatigue progression during prolonged repetitive tasks until voluntary fatigue onset; fatigue-labeled training datasets were collected from subjects performing sustained flexion-extension cycles under controlled experimental conditions.

For RF, optimal parameters were reported as ntree = 1000 and mtry = 16.'}
{'sample_description': 'The study used data from the ReaSTOC database, an ongoing prospective physiological and biomedical signal data warehousing project including all consecutive adult patients admitted to the medical ICU of CHRU de Brest (ClinicalTrials.gov identifier NCT02893462). Patients were enrolled within the first 24 hours of ICU admission. Inclusion required availability of high-fidelity photoplethysmography (PPG) signals recorded for 2 consecutive hours in native resolution (75 Hz), with patients positioned supine with 30° upper-body angulation. Exclusion criteria included explicit refusal by the patient and/or relatives to participate. Clinical metadata—including history of diabetes, beta-blocker treatment, ICU/hospital length-of-stay, survival status at discharge and Day 28, need for respiratory assistance, and hemodynamic instability (defined as fluid challenge ≥0.5 L crystalloids and/or initiation/modification of vasopressors within the prior 2 hours)—were collected via standard monitoring devices (Intellivue MP70® and ICCA® Philips Healthcare) and the computerized medical file. A composite prognosis index was predefined as: (1) death, (2) need for any form of respiratory assistance during ICU stay, and/or (3) ICU length-of-stay >7 days (median duration).', 'kNN_configuration_description': 'k-Nearest Neighbors (kNN) was employed as part of a feature ranking procedure performed via discriminant analysis with cross-validation using artificial intelligence and neural networks. The kNN implementation was integrated into supervised machine learning model evaluation alongside other classifiers (subspace discriminant, fine Gaussian SVM, logistic regression, decision trees, K-Means, and neural networks). Although the exact value of k is not explicitly stated, the method specifies that kNN was used in conjunction with cross-validation for discriminant analysis and feature selection; performance metrics—including Positive Predictive Value (PPV), Negative Predictive Value (NPV), sensitivity (Se), and specificity (Sp)—were calculated a posteriori when applying the decision tree model to the real database. Analyses involving kNN were conducted using MATLAB (MathWorks, Natick, MA, USA).', 'process_procedure_description': 'Physiological PPG signals were acquired using a dedicated software called Synapse (LTSI INSERM UMR 1099) over a 2-hour window within the first 24 h of ICU admission. HRV analysis was performed on PPG tracings using Kubios HRV Premium 3.0 (Kuopio, Finland) following standard methods: for each HRV marker, the average of three non-overlapping data samples—each at least 3 minutes long—was computed across the 2-hour recording. Time-domain features included R-R intervals, RMSSD, and triangular index; frequency-domain analysis used discrete Fourier transform to compute power in HF (0.15–0.40 Hz), LF (0.04–0.15 Hz), VLF (0.0033–0.04 Hz) bands and LF/HF ratio; non-linear analysis included Poincaré plot-derived SD1 and SD2, plus wavelet entropy measures (Approximate Entropy, Sample Entropy, Shannon Entropy). Statistical analysis was conducted using MedCalc v17.8 (Ostend, Belgium) for univariate/multivariate tests (Kruskal–Wallis for continuous variables, Fisher’s exact test for categorical variables) and ROC curve analysis to determine optimal HRV thresholds (requiring ≥60% sensitivity and specificity). Feature ranking and machine learning modeling—including kNN, SVM, logistic regression, decision trees, K-Means, and neural networks—were implemented in MATLAB. Decision tree performance was validated by calculating PPV, NPV, Se, and Sp on the real ReaSTOC database.'}
{'sample_description': 'The study used raw tremor data from six healthy subjects and seven multiple sclerosis (MS) patients. Healthy subjects were randomly selected from hospital staff at Vancouver General Hospital, Canada, with an average age of 45 years and no history of neuromuscular disease. MS patients were randomly selected from those referred to the same hospital and undergoing unilateral or bilateral deep brain stimulation (DBS); they had an average age of 47 years and included both genders. All participants performed a modified Finger-to-Nose Test using the Optotrak™ motion analysis system: markers were placed on the tip of the index finger (left and right hands) and on two fixed locations on a desk (at ~10 cm and ~40 cm from the subject’s body).

Decision tree performance was validated by calculating PPV, NPV, Se, and Sp on the real ReaSTOC database.'}
{'sample_description': 'The study used raw tremor data from six healthy subjects and seven multiple sclerosis (MS) patients. Healthy subjects were randomly selected from hospital staff at Vancouver General Hospital, Canada, with an average age of 45 years and no history of neuromuscular disease. MS patients were randomly selected from those referred to the same hospital and undergoing unilateral or bilateral deep brain stimulation (DBS); they had an average age of 47 years and included both genders. All participants performed a modified Finger-to-Nose Test using the Optotrak™ motion analysis system: markers were placed on the tip of the index finger (left and right hands) and on two fixed locations on a desk (at ~10 cm and ~40 cm from the subject’s body). Participants repeatedly touched the farther marker and then the nearer marker in back-and-forth movements. Healthy subjects completed three trials at 10-minute intervals; MS patients completed six trials—three with DBS ON and three with DBS OFF. The horizontal position of the index finger marker perpendicular to the line between the two fixed markers was extracted as the tremor signal.', 'kNN_configuration_description': 'The study employed a Weighted K-Nearest Neighbor (Weighted KNN) classifier for tremor signal classification into three groups: healthy, MS patients with DBS ON, and MS patients with DBS OFF. The optimal number of neighbors (K) was empirically determined to be five. Distance weighting was applied such that not all K neighbors contributed equally; instead, closer neighbors were assigned higher weights. The City Block (Manhattan) distance metric was used to compute distances in the feature space. The classifier was evaluated using ten-fold cross-validation to ensure robustness and avoid overfitting. No additional preprocessing (e.g., feature scaling) is explicitly mentioned, but the method is noted to be less sensitive to class imbalance and independent of feature count or dataset length.', 'process_procedure_description': "The analysis pipeline consisted of several software-supported computational steps: (1) Signal preprocessing: mean removal (bias correction) followed by Butterworth lowpass filtering (cutoff frequency = 7 Hz) to remove artifacts; (2) Dysmetria separation: Empirical Mode Decomposition (EMD) was applied to decompose the tremor signal into Intrinsic Mode Functions (IMFs), and Hilbert Transform (HT) was used to obtain time-frequency representations; dysmetria timing was identified from movement onset, and a Butterworth highpass filter (cutoff = 2 Hz) was applied to isolate tremor from dysmetria; (3) Feature extraction: nine features were computed per hand and condition (DBS ON/OFF/healthy), including three entropy measures (Approximate Entropy, Sample Entropy, Fuzzy Entropy), wavelet energy (using wavelet decomposition up to 'n' levels, with energy defined as the sum of squared detail coefficients per level), statistical features (distance average to direct path, deviation from direct path), and first-IMF-specific features (energy, dominant frequency via Welch’s PSD with Hamming windows of length 256 and 50% segment overlap, maximum amplitude); (4) Statistical analysis: Wilcoxon Signed Rank test (for DBS ON vs. OFF), Mann-Whitney U test (for group comparisons involving healthy controls), and repeated-measures ANOVA (non-parametric) for trial repeatability; (5) Classification: three classifiers—Support Vector Machine (with Gaussian and Quadratic kernels), Neural Network (two-layer feed-forward MLP with sigmoid hidden layer and softmax output layer, trained via scaled conjugate gradient backpropagation), and Weighted KNN—were applied; all used ten-fold cross-validation; performance was compared using Receiver Operating Characteristic (ROC) curves with sensitivity and specificity metrics."}
{'sample_description': 'Eight able-bodied (AB) subjects (three female and five male), all right-hand dominant, aged 21–24 years, with forearm circumferences ranging from 22.8 to 28.6 cm (mean ± SD: 25.2 ± 2.2 cm), were recruited. All participants provided written informed consent and approval was obtained from the Institutional Review Board of Shenzhen Institute of Advanced Technology, Chinese Academy of Sciences. Each subject performed six motion classes—hand close (HC), hand open (HO), wrist pronation (WP), wrist supination (WS), wrist extension (WE), and wrist flexion (WF)—30 times each, with 2-second rests between repetitions and 2-minute rests after every 10 repetitions to mitigate muscle fatigue.

For multivariable modeling: generalized linear mixed models (GLMMs) with random intercepts (to account for repeated IVF cycles per woman) were fitted using Poisson distribution/log link for oocyte counts, normal distribution/identity link for endometrial thickness, and binomial distribution/logit link for binary outcomes (fertilization, implantation, clinical pregnancy, live birth); population marginal means were reported after adjustment for confounders (age, BMI, year of IVF cycle, SART infertility diagnosis, prior IUI at MGH, and scores of the other two PCA-derived factors). BKMR models included hierarchical variable selection, kernel-based smooth functional representation of exposures, and adjustment for the same confounders (excluding the other PCA factor scores). Sensitivity analyses included restricting to 2006–2012 cycles, cycles with two urine samples, first IVF cycle per woman, and excluding cryo-thaw cycles; stratified analyses assessed effect modification by age (<37 vs. ≥37), BMI (<25 vs. ≥25 kg/m²), and protocol type (agonist vs. other), with p<0.10 considered evidence of interaction. All tests were two-tailed; p<0.05 was deemed statistically significant."}
{'sample_description': 'The study used 71 serum samples collected from 50-year-old male patients undergoing prostate biopsy for prostate cancer (PCa) diagnosis at the Urology Unit of MultiMedica Castellanza, Varese, Italy. Samples were stratified into three groups: 31 healthy controls (28 with total PSA < 2.5 ng/mL and 3 with total PSA < 4 ng/mL and free-PSA > 15%), 20 patients diagnosed with PCa (confirmed by histological analysis of biopsy), and 20 subjects with benign prostate hyperplasia (BPH). Exclusion criteria included autoimmune diseases, hypersensitivities, and other immune-mediated physical states. Blood was collected in siliconized tubes, allowed to coagulate for 30 min, then centrifuged at 3,000 rpm for 30 min at 4°C; sera were aliquoted into 1.8-mL cryotubes and stored at −80°C until analysis. For steroid quantification, 10 µL of isotopically labeled internal standard mixture (containing Aldosterone-d8, Androstenedione-13C3, Corticosterone-d4, Cortisol-d4, 11-Deoxycortisol-d5, DHEA-d6, Progesterone-13C3, 17-OH-Progesterone-13C3, DHEAS-d6, Testosterone-13C3) was added to 290 µL serum, followed by 600 µL precipitating reagent E, vortexing, and centrifugation at 13,000 × g for 1 min. The resulting 700 µL supernatant was dried under nitrogen gas and resuspended in 70 µL resuspension reagent D. Analyte calibrants (aldosterone, corticosterone, cortisol, 11-deoxycortisol, androstenedione, testosterone, DHEA, DHEAS, 17-OH-progesterone, progesterone) and quality controls were processed identically. Stability, LOD, and LOQ were assessed using calibration curves (7-point) and kit-provided controls.', 'Bayesian PCA_configuration_description': '', 'process_procedure_description': ''}
{'sample_description': 'The study used two types of synthetic gene expression datasets generated by GeneNetWeaver: one simulating Escherichia coli (E. coli) and another simulating yeast. For initial evaluation, 10 distinct GRNs were generated for each organism, each containing 30 genes and 41 time points. To further assess scalability and robustness, additional synthetic datasets were generated with larger network sizes: 10 networks per organism, each with 50 genes and 101 time points. All synthetic datasets were time-series microarray expression data designed to mimic realistic transcriptional dynamics, including regulatory time lags and noise. The purpose of generating multiple replicates (10 per condition) was to mitigate biases arising from idiosyncratic network topologies or stochastic variations in network generation.', 'Bayesian PCA_configuration_description': 'The paper does not use Bayesian PCA; it explicitly distinguishes between standard Principal Component Analysis (PCA) and the Bayesian Information Criterion (BIC) as two *separate*, non-Bayesian approaches for determining the number of hidden variables (m) in the State Space Model (SSM).

Additional annotation cross-referenced HMDB (https://hmdb.ca/) and KEGG (https://www.genome.jp/kegg) databases. Pathway analysis was conducted using MetaboAnalyst 4.0 (http://www.metaboanalyst.ca/), HMDB, and iPath v3 (https://pathways.embl.de/). Statistical analyses included ANOVA with Bonferroni/Dunn post hoc tests for glycogen and triglyceride concentrations, and multivariate modeling (PCA/PLS-DA) in SIMCA 17. Data visualization and simplified metabolic mapping were performed manually by the first author based on KEGG pathway maps.'}
{'sample_description': "The study used fecal samples from human participants and mice. Human participants included 5 groups: normal control (NC) group (n=25), Crohn's disease without antibiotics administration (CD) group (n=12), Crohn's disease with antibiotics administration (CD-A) group (n=12), ulcerative colitis without antibiotics administration (UC) group (n=19), and ulcerative colitis with antibiotics administration (UC-A) group (n=19), totaling 87 human subjects recruited from Tongji Hospital, Huazhong University of Science and Technology, Wuhan, China. Fecal samples were collected for targeted metabolomics of bile acids and 16S rRNA sequencing. Additionally, fecal samples from 6-week-old C57BL/6 female mice (n=15 total across experimental groups) were used, including DSS-induced acute colitis models treated with ceftriaxone sodium (CRO), vancomycin (VA), VA + imipenem (IPM), or vehicle control, with some groups also receiving lithocholic acid (LCA) supplementation; all mouse fecal samples were stored at –80\u202f°C prior to analysis.", 'SIMCA_configuration_description': 'Multivariate data analysis of bile acid profiling was performed using SIMCA (Umetrics, Sweden). Specifically, Principal Component Analysis (PCA) and Orthogonal Partial Least Squares Discriminant Analysis (OPLS-DA) were conducted in SIMCA. Variable Importance in Projection (VIP) analysis was implemented within the OPLS-DA model to identify bile acids with the highest discriminative power among the five human groups (NC, CD, CD-A, UC, UC-A); a VIP threshold of 1 was applied to select significantly contributing bile acids (e.g., TCDCA, TCA, DCA, LCA, βMCA). The method section does not specify additional SIMCA parameters such as number of components, cross-validation settings, scaling method (e.g., Pareto scaling or unit variance), or preprocessing steps applied within SIMCA, but confirms use of standard SIMCA v16 or similar (implied by reference to Umetrics and standard multivariate workflows).', 'process_procedure_description': 'The process procedure for multivariate statistical analysis of bile acid data involved: (1) importing preprocessed, normalized bile acid concentration data (14 bile acids quantified via UPLC-MS in human and mouse feces) into SIMCA software (Umetrics, Sweden); (2) performing unsupervised Principal Component Analysis (PCA) to assess overall clustering and variance distribution among the five human groups; (3) conducting supervised Orthogonal Partial Least Squares Discriminant Analysis (OPLS-DA) to maximize separation between predefined classes (NC, CD, CD-A, UC, UC-A); (4) extracting Variable Importance in Projection (VIP) scores from the OPLS-DA model to rank bile acids by their contribution to group discrimination, using a VIP threshold of 1.0 to identify the most influential metabolites. No other software tools (e.g., R packages or Python libraries) were mentioned for this specific multivariate analysis step; SIMCA was explicitly named as the sole platform for PCA, OPLS-DA, and VIP analysis.'}
{'sample_description': 'The study used nine tobacco extract samples (labeled 1-9) prepared from American tobacco leaves (upper tobacco B2F, middle tobacco C2F, and lower tobacco C3F) sourced from Henan Zhongyan Industrial Co., Ltd. Samples 1–5 were prepared by extracting five 20 g portions of crushed tobacco in 200 g of 60% ethanol via heated reflux for durations ranging from 4 to 12 h (in 2 h increments), followed by filtration and vacuum concentration. Samples 6–8 were obtained from three sequential 12 h heated reflux extractions of 50 kg crushed tobacco in 500 kg of 60% ethanol/water solution.

LOQs were determined as 10× noise from 11 blank filter measurements. Repeatability (RSD <12%) and reproducibility (RSD <17%) were assessed using Seronorm™ Level II DBSs. Statistical analyses (univariate, correlation, PCA, CA, LDA) were carried out using Statgraphics Centurion XVII-X64, while SIMCA was performed using THE UNSCRAMBLER 7.01 — though no specific SIMCA parameterization is described.'}
{'sample_description': 'Urine samples were collected from db/db mice after 3 weeks of treatment with total flavonoids from Selaginella tamariscina (TFST) at high (220 mg/kg) and low (110 mg/kg) doses, gliclazide (24 mg/kg), or vehicle (water); normal control (db/m) and diabetic control (db/db) groups were also included. Urine samples were thawed on ice-water, centrifuged at 4 °C for 10 min at 3500 r/min, and 500 µL supernatant was mixed with 1500 µL cold acetonitrile; the mixture was vortexed for 3 min, then centrifuged at 12000 r/min for 10 min; 1 mL of the resulting supernatant was transferred to sample vials for UPLC-Q/TOF-MS analysis.', 'SIMCA_configuration_description': 'Principal component analysis (PCA) was performed using SIMCA-P 13.0 (Umetrics AB, Muea, Sweden); the software was used to process metabolomics data after peak alignment and normalization with ProfileAnalysis 2.1 (Bruker, Germany); no additional specific parameter settings (e.g., scaling method, number of components, cross-validation details) beyond the version identifier and default analytical purpose (unsupervised PCA) were explicitly stated in the text.', 'process_procedure_description': "Metabolomics data processing involved raw data correction (background noise subtraction and peak alignment) followed by normalization using ProfileAnalysis 2.1 (Bruker, Germany); subsequent multivariate statistical analysis included unsupervised principal component analysis (PCA) performed in SIMCA-P 13.0 (Umetrics AB, Muea, Sweden) and supervised orthogonal partial least squares discriminant analysis (OPLS-DA) for biomarker identification; potential biomarkers were filtered using variable importance in projection (VIP) values >4 and Student's t-test P < 0.05; statistical significance for biochemical and Western blot data was assessed using one-way ANOVA in SPSS 18.0 (IBM, New York, NY, USA) with P < 0.05 considered statistically significant."}
{'sample_description': 'The study used plasma samples from three groups: 19 papillary thyroid carcinoma (PTC) patients (12 female, 7 male; mean age 35.37 ± 10.80 years), 16 multinodular goiter (MNG) patients (12 female, 4 male; mean age 52.06 ± 10.96 years), and 20 healthy volunteers (8 female, 12 male; mean age 43.4 ± 14.33 years) with normal TSH (0.4–4.0 mIU/L) and no thyroid-related disorders. Blood (5 mL) was drawn pre-operatively from patients (before any drug or iodine therapy) and from healthy subjects in the morning after overnight fasting, using EDTA-containing plasma-collecting tubes. Plasma was immediately separated by centrifugation at 3,000 rpm for 10 min at 4°C, aliquoted into 1.5 mL Eppendorf tubes, and stored at −80°C until analysis. Metabolite extraction involved adding 1 mL methanol/water/isopropanol (5:2:2, v/v/v) to 50 μL plasma, vortexing, chilling at −20°C for 20 min, centrifuging at 14,000 rpm for 15 min at 4°C, and drying the supernatant under vacuum centrifugation at 45°C for 3 h. Derivatization consisted of methoximation (30 μL of 20 mg/mL methoxyamine hydrochloride in pyridine, 60°C, 900 rpm, 1 h) followed by trimethylsilylation (60 μL MSTFA, 45°C, 900 rpm, 20 min).', 'SIMCA_configuration_description': 'SIMCA-P 15.0 software (Umetrics, Umeå, Sweden) was used to construct multivariate statistical models. Principal component analysis (PCA) was performed for data overview and outlier detection.

All tools operate synchronously on the current spectrum, with real-time color-coded annotations (e.g., red for b-ions, blue for y-ions) in the main visualization panel.'}
{'sample_description': 'The sample used in the research consists of electron ionization (EI) mass spectra of small molecules, where each spectrum contains peaks characterized by m/z values and intensities. The model operates on spectral data interpreted as assemblies of rAEs (reconstructed Atom Environments)—disconnected substructures representing atom-type compositions of fragment ions. Molecular structures are derived from these rAEs without requiring the presence of an observed molecular ion peak; instead, the molecular rAEs are either inferred from contextual spectral evidence or directly derived from the known molecular structure when ground-truth information is available. The training data includes EI-MS spectra paired with corresponding molecular structures, enabling learning of peak-to-fragment and fragment-to-molecule relationships.', 'Cosine_configuration_description': '', 'process_procedure_description': "The method employs a custom transformer-based deep learning model implemented using PyTorch. Input tokens consist of m/z values, intensity indicators ('i'), and logarithmic intensity ranks (logRank), which range from 1 to 7 and are adapted from Cao and Guler (2021) to reduce parameter count and prevent overfitting. The transformer decoder architecture processes these inputs using self-attention mechanisms with positional encodings—applied despite permutation invariance of rAE groupings—to improve training stability and performance. The target output is tokenized as sequences of rAE blocks (each block representing atom-type groupings from individual fragment ions), followed by a special <mol> token demarcating the transition to molecular-level rAEs; all rAE groupings are sorted alphabetically before input to ensure consistency. The model learns contextual relationships across the entire spectrum rather than enforcing one-to-one peak-to-fragment mappings, enabling inference of molecular atom-type content through aggregated, decomposed, and organized rAE predictions. Post-processing converts predicted rAE sequences into the final atom-type composition of the molecule. Model training setup and hyperparameters are detailed in the Supplementary Methods, and the source code and trained models are publicly available at https://github.com/snu-lcbc/MassSpec-RefAIner and Zenodo (10.5281/zenodo.17075492)."}
{'sample_description': 'The dataset used in this study consists of 3728 Japanese-language radiology reports from plain head CT examinations conducted at Osaka University Hospital. These reports were categorized into five Report Importance Categories (RIC) by neuroradiologists based on clinical significance: Category 0 (no findings, 15.0%), Category 1 (minor findings, 26.7%), Category 2 (routine follow-up, 44.2%), Category 3 (careful follow-up, 7.7%), and Category 4 (examination or therapy, 6.4%). The dataset was split into training (2385 reports, 64%), validation (597 reports, 16%), and test (746 reports, 20%) sets using random sampling, preserving the original class imbalance across splits.', 'Cosine_configuration_description': 'Cosine distance (CD) was employed as one of two distance-based active learning (AL) algorithms. For each unlabeled report x in the unlabeled data pool (UDP), the UTH-BERT model generated a 768-dimensional vector v_x. Category center vectors (v_c) were precomputed for each of the five RIC categories by averaging the UTH-BERT embeddings of all labeled reports belonging to that category in the labeled dataset (LD). The cosine distance φ_x for report x was calculated as φ_x = 1 − min_c [ (v_x · v_c) / (||v_x|| × ||v_c||) ], i.e., 1 minus the maximum cosine similarity between v_x and any category center vector v_c. Reports with the highest φ_x (i.e., lowest cosine similarity to all category centers) were selected for labeling. The granularity parameter N was fixed to 25 reports per AL iteration.

(2006) formula: ITR = [log₂N + P log₂P + (1−P) log₂((1−P)/(N−1))]/T, where N = 26 (letters), P = classification accuracy, T = time per character selection in minutes.'}
{'sample_description': 'The study utilized two primary experimental systems: (1) The Catalyst™ optical surface imaging (OSI) system (C-RAD, Sweden), specifically its cRespiratory module, for real-time motion tracking and gating in radiotherapy; the system employed three cameras operating at a sampling frequency >15 Hz, with camera parameters and scanning volume adjusted per clinical requirements; (2) A modified QUASAR programmable respiratory motion phantom (Modus Medical Devices, London, ON, Canada), vertically oriented on a translation stage to simulate anterior–posterior (AP) directional respiratory motion with controllable amplitude variation; a white detection plate reinforced by two stents was added to enhance surface visibility for optical tracking. Additionally, clinical respiratory signals from 13 anonymized patients—categorized into regular and irregular patterns—were acquired and imported into the phantom for validation. For dosimetric evaluation, Gafchromic EBT3 film (Ashland ISP Advanced Materials, NJ, USA) was used, placed between two 2-cm-thick equivalent water slabs for dose build-up and backscatter, and irradiated under a 2×2 cm², 6 MV photon beam (500 MU) on Elekta Versa-HD and Edge linear accelerators at isocenter.', 'Cosine_configuration_description': '', 'process_procedure_description': 'The experimental methodology involved multiple interdependent procedures: (1) Camera thermal drift assessment: Catalyst™ cameras were powered on in temperature-stable conditions; respiratory curves were continuously recorded, and thermal stabilization time and drift magnitude were determined via quadratic function fitting and derivative analysis of positional data over time; (2) Cosine respiratory curve tracking accuracy testing: The QUASAR phantom generated motion using Z(t) = −b × cos(6πt/τ + π/2), where amplitude b ranged from 3 mm to 15 mm (2-mm increments) and period τ from 1 s to 10 s (1-s increments); OSI-recorded output curves were compared against theoretical inputs using adjusted R² (A_R²) and root mean square error (RMSE); (3) Clinical respiratory curve tracking validation: Patient-derived respiratory signals were input into the phantom, and Catalyst™-recorded motion curves were Fourier-transformed to eliminate phase/spatial misalignment artifacts; similarity was quantified using Pearson correlation coefficient (PCC) in the frequency domain; (4) Respiratory-gating time delay measurement: Gating signals were simulated using a uniformly accelerated motion formula y₁ = a × (t − T/2)² + b for the translation stage, while Catalyst™-monitored motion followed y₂ = Min − (Max − Min)/T² × (t − T/2)² + Max; beam-on/off delays (t_on’, t_off’) were derived by applying a dose convolution-fitting method to EBT3 film dosimetry data—where static dose profile f(x) was convolved with a Dirac delta train g(x) representing pulse-by-pulse beam positions xₙ computed via Eq. 4; least-squares optimization over 10-ms intervals identified optimal t_on’ and t_off’; (5) Film dosimetry protocol: EBT3 films were scanned 24 h post-irradiation using an Epson 11000 XL scanner at 150 dpi in 48-bit RGB TIFF format (professional mode, no correction); dose maps were generated using FilmQAPro® software (ISP Advanced Materials); (6) Linac-specific testing: Beam hold times ranged from 409 ms to 4264 ms; Versa-HD and Edge linacs were tested with 8-s and 6-s motion periods respectively; all measurements adhered to TG-142 compliance criteria for gating latency.'}
{'sample_description': 'The study used a cohort of 2930 men aged 65 years or older from the Osteoporotic Fractures in Men Study (MrOS) sleep ancillary study, recruited between 2003 and 2005 across six U.S. clinical centers (Birmingham, AL; Minneapolis, MN; Palo Alto, CA; Monongahela Valley near Pittsburgh, PA; Portland, OR; and San Diego, CA). Participants wore wrist-worn actigraphs (SleepWatch-O; Ambulatory Monitoring, Inc.) continuously for at least three 24-hour periods to collect rest-activity rhythm (RAR) data from December 1, 2003, to March 31, 2005.

The resulting EMG data matrix (muscles × time points) was submitted to NNMF to extract synergies; the number of synergies was determined as the minimum k such that variance accounted for per muscle (VAF_m) > 0.9 and global VAF (VAF_g) > 0.8. Weight matrices (W) were then compared to reference W matrices from an able-bodied control using Cosine Similarity, with merging and undefined classification rules applied as described. Weighting differences (Wd = Wt − Wr) were computed after scaling each synergy’s muscle weights to unit maximum to enable valid subtraction; thresholds of |Wd| ≤ 0.3 indicated close-to-normal weighting patterns.'}
{'sample_description': 'The study uses four benchmark drug-target interaction (DTI) datasets derived from DrugBank, SuperTarget, BRENDA, and KEGG BRITE: Enzymes (445 drugs, 664 target proteins, 2926 experimentally verified DTIs), Ion Channels (210 drugs, 204 targets, 1476 DTIs), GPCRs (223 drugs, 95 targets, 635 DTIs), and Nuclear Receptors (54 drugs, 26 targets, 90 DTIs). Each dataset is represented as a bipartite network where drugs and proteins are nodes and known DTIs are positive edges. Due to extreme class imbalance (e.g., in GPCRs: 635 positives vs. ~20,550 potential negatives), negative samples are generated via down-sampling—selecting an equal number of non-interacting drug–protein pairs as positives. All negative samples are assumed to be true negatives with negligible contamination from unverified interactions, justified by statistical improbability given the large negative-to-positive ratio. The final training data consists of balanced binary labeled pairs (positive = known DTI; negative = down-sampled non-DTI pair) for each dataset.', 'Cosine_configuration_description': '', 'process_procedure_description': 'The method employs a multi-stage computational pipeline: (1) Drug molecular characterization: PubChem-based molecular fingerprints encoding 881 chemical substructures into binary 881-dimensional vectors; (2) Protein sequence numerical characterization: Position-Specific Scoring Matrix (PSSM) generated using PSI-BLAST against the expert-curated SwissProt database with e-value = 0.001 and 3 iterations, yielding L × 20 matrices (L = protein length); (3) Feature extraction: Discrete Cosine Transform (DCT) applied to the PSSM matrix to compress evolutionary information; the first 400 DCT coefficients are selected as the final protein feature descriptor; (4) Classification prediction: Rotation Forest (RF) classifier used for DTI prediction, composed of L = 42 decision trees, each trained on K = 21 rotated and transformed feature subsets; RF parameters optimized via grid search using accuracy as objective; (5) Evaluation: 5-fold cross-validation performed on each dataset; performance assessed using Accuracy, Sensitivity, Precision, Matthews Correlation Coefficient (MCC), and AUC; ROC curves generated for all four datasets.'}
{'sample_description': 'The study used HCD (Higher-Energy Collisional Dissociation) and ETD (Electron-Transfer Dissociation) MS/MS spectra from four publicly available spectral libraries: the NIST HCD library, the NIST Synthetic HCD library, the Human HCD library from MassIVE, and the synthetic HCD library from ProteomeTools. For HCD, training data included ~1.5 million 2+ and ~1 million 3+ spectra (totaling ~2.2 million), while testing data consisted of ~16,000 2+ and ~14,000 3+ spectra, with no peptide sequence overlap between training and testing sets. Additional testing sets were constructed for 1+, 4+, and ETD spectra: 1,262 1+ HCD, 5,540 4+ HCD, 4,666 2+ ETD, 17,208 3+ ETD, and 3,405 4+ ETD spectra. All datasets were rigorously pre-processed: under-fragmented and over-fragmented PSMs (<1% of total) were removed; precursor mass tolerance was restricted to ≤200 ppm; and precursor peaks were explicitly removed from each spectrum. Spectra were normalized by dividing each spectrum’s intensities by its maximum peak intensity. Peptide sequences were limited to unmodified, up to 25 residues long, and encoded as a 27×23 matrix containing one-hot encoding (20 amino acids + end/pad tokens) in rows 1–22 and monoisotopic amino acid masses in row 27.

0.5 mL/min), and 'quantity-effect' potency calculation using verbascoside calibration (y = 2E-07x⁴ − 8E-05x³ + 0.0079x² + 0.5755x + 1.4754, R² = 1) to quantify antioxidant potencies relative to 1 μg/mL verbascoside = 1 unit."}
{'sample_description': 'The study used two types of samples: (1) human subjects and (2) a physical phantom. For human subjects, six healthy volunteers (4 male, 2 female; age range 32–59 years) were recruited under an IRB-approved protocol. All subjects were scanned on a 3T MRI scanner (Signa MR750, General Electric Healthcare) equipped with the MAGNUS head-gradient system (G_max = 200 mT/m, SR_max = 500 T/m/s per-axis), with whole-body gradient and RF coil removed. A custom 37-cm inner-diameter RF transmit/receiver coil was used for transmission, and a 32-channel phased-array brain coil (Nova Medical) served as the receiver. The phantom was a spherical (11-cm diameter) container filled with 20% polyvinylpyrrolidone (PVP) solution, having a nominal mean diffusivity of ~1350 μm²/s at room temperature; it was placed at scanner isocenter for 30 minutes prior to scanning to minimize thermal conduction and residual motion effects.', 'Cosine_configuration_description': '', 'process_procedure_description': 'The research employed a custom-modified spin-echo diffusion-EPI sequence on a 3T MRI scanner (Signa MR750, GE Healthcare) to implement oscillating gradient spin-echo (OGSE) diffusion encoding. The diffusion encoding waveform duration was fixed at τ = 50 ms per lobe (i.e., total symmetric pair duration = 100 ms), with waveforms applied before and after a 7-ms refocusing pulse. Five OGSE frequencies were tested: 20, 40, 60, 80, and 100 Hz. For each frequency, one b=0 s/mm² and twenty diffusion-weighted directions (b=450 s/mm²) were acquired; identical gradient directions were used across all frequencies. PGSE (Stejskal-Tanner) controls matched b-values using gradients of ~50 ms duration (mixing time = 11.1 ms, pulse width = 4.1 ms). Additional reduced-direction scans (12 directions per frequency) and parallel imaging (in-plane acceleration factor = 2) were acquired in two subjects to assess SNR and acquisition time trade-offs. Acquisition parameters included TR/TE = 7000/122 ms, FOV = 25.6 cm, matrix = 128×128, slice thickness = 3 mm, 42 slices, no gap, echo spacing = 428 μs. Gradient nonlinearity correction was applied for MAGNUS head-gradient data using an in-house implementation and ANTs-based methods. DTI metrics (MD, RD, PD, FA) were computed per frequency using standard tensor fitting with shared b=0 image. White matter parcels were defined via non-rigid registration (ANTs SyN) of individual FA maps to the JHU ICBM DTI-81 atlas (50 regions), and gray matter parcels were derived from a 116-region MNI atlas grouped into 7 larger regions. Linear fitting of the short-range disorder model D(ω) = D_∞ + Aπ²|ω|⁻¹ (where ω = 2πf) was performed both region-wise (using mean values per parcel) and voxel-wise. Statistical analysis included Wilcoxon signed-rank tests (per parcel, p<0.05) for OGSE vs. PGSE comparisons and paired t-tests for direction-count and parallel imaging effects, with coefficient of variation (CoV) also compared. Phantom analysis used a central rectangular ROI (~15 cm²) for diffusivity quantification.'}
{'sample_description': 'Twenty healthy individuals (11 female; mean age 24.3 ± 3.1 years) provided informed consent to participate in the study, which was approved by the NIH Combined Central Nervous System Institutional Review Board. All experiments were performed in accordance with the Declaration of Helsinki.', 'Cosine_configuration_description': "A modified cosine similarity method was used to measure between-condition similarity and within-condition repeatability in evoked topographical activity patterns. Prior to analysis, data were Hjorth-transformed to reduce the influence of volume conduction. For each condition, an average waveform was created from 50 randomly-selected trials per participant.

All computational steps—including binning, smoothing, metric computation, and matrix visualization—were executed using self-written MATLAB software (no third-party toolkits or commercial packages specified); source code is available upon request. The method was validated across multiple tissue types and optimized for ambient tissue profiling applications requiring robust artifact rejection prior to automated classification.'}

{'sample_description': 'The sample consisted of 10 adults (aged 19.8–32.7 years, mean = 23.5 years) and 40 children with normal hearing sensitivity (pure-tone thresholds ≤20 dB HL at octave frequencies from 250 to 8000 Hz, per ANSI 2010). Children ranged in age from 5.2 to 15.1 years (mean = 8.7 years), with age distribution approximately uniform on the logarithm of age to reflect decelerating maturation; one child (9.6 years) was excluded due to evidence of flagging attention (progressive worsening of thresholds by factor of 10 across replicates), and four children (5.8, 6.8, 7.4, and 15.1 years) had previously participated in Buss et al. (2014) with ≥4 months between experiments. All stimuli were generated at a 12207-Hz sampling rate; noise samples were 10.7 s long (2^17 points), played continuously at 70 dB SPL without discontinuities except for imposed gaps, and a new noise sample was generated before each threshold estimation track.', 'Cosine_configuration_description': 'Raised cosine ramps were used to introduce temporal gaps in stimuli: 40-ms raised cosines for low-fluctuation 25-Hz-wide narrowband noise (centered at 500 or 5000 Hz), and either 4-ms or 40-ms raised cosines for wideband Gaussian noise (500–5000 Hz, 4500-Hz bandwidth). Gap duration was defined as the interval between the beginning of stimulus offset and the beginning of subsequent onset; when gap duration was less than ramp duration, offset and onset overlapped such that stimulus amplitude at the center of the gap did not fall to zero. For analysis of level-change criteria, idealized envelopes were aligned at gap midpoints, and intersection levels (e.g., 7.8-dB down for youngest children, 3.0-dB down for adults) were computed to estimate criterion level changes.', 'process_procedure_description': 'Threshold estimation was controlled using a custom MATLAB script that generated noise stimuli and loaded them into a real-time processor (RP2, Tucker-Davis Technologies, Alachua, FL); stimuli were routed via a headphone buffer (HB7, TDT) and presented monaurally to the left ear using Sennheiser HD 265 circumaural headphones inside a double-walled sound-attenuating booth. A three-alternative forced-choice (3AFC) procedure with a two-down, one-up adaptive stepping rule was employed to estimate the gap duration corresponding to 71% correct performance. Initial step size was a factor of 1.41 in gap duration, reduced to 1.19 after the second track reversal. Each track terminated after eight reversals; threshold estimate per track was the geometric mean of the final six reversals. At least three threshold estimates were collected per condition; a fourth was added if the first three differed by >factor of 1.5. Final threshold per condition was the geometric mean of all obtained estimates. Stimulus intervals were 500 ms with 500-ms interstimulus intervals; visual response cues were displayed on a touch-sensitive monitor, with gamified feedback (jigsaw puzzle animation, progress bar, cartoon reward). Conditions were presented in random order, with all tracks for a given condition completed before proceeding to the next. Maximum allowable gap duration was 500 ms (never reached). Data analyses were performed on log-transformed gap durations and log-transformed age; nonparametric statistics (e.g., Spearman rank-order correlations, univariate ANOVA on threshold ratios) were applied where appropriate due to heteroscedasticity and individual variability.'}
{'sample_description': 'Human myelogenous leukemia K562 cells were purchased from ATCC and cultured in DMEM supplemented with 10% FBS, 50 U/ml penicillin, and 50 μg/ml streptomycin under standard conditions (37 °C, 5% CO₂, humidified incubator). Cells were collected, washed with PBS, fixed with 4% paraformaldehyde for ~2 hours, then divided into three groups: vehicle (control), low saponin (0.025% w/v), and high saponin (0.05% w/v).

Maximum allowable gap duration was 500 ms (never reached). Data analyses were performed on log-transformed gap durations and log-transformed age; nonparametric statistics (e.g., Spearman rank-order correlations, univariate ANOVA on threshold ratios) were applied where appropriate due to heteroscedasticity and individual variability.'}
{'sample_description': 'Human myelogenous leukemia K562 cells were purchased from ATCC and cultured in DMEM supplemented with 10% FBS, 50 U/ml penicillin, and 50 μg/ml streptomycin under standard conditions (37 °C, 5% CO₂, humidified incubator). Cells were collected, washed with PBS, fixed with 4% paraformaldehyde for ~2 hours, then divided into three groups: vehicle (control), low saponin (0.025% w/v), and high saponin (0.05% w/v). Saponin was added to fixed cell suspensions (~10 million cells/ml) to reach final concentrations; incubation lasted 30 min at room temperature. Approximately 30 million cells per group were centrifuged at 6000 g for 2 min in 0.65 mL Eppendorf tubes to form pellets; supernatant was removed. Estimated intracellular volume fraction was ~72%. Six samples per group were prepared. Cell size was confirmed via phase contrast microscopy on glass slides; >100 cells per sample measured, yielding radii of 9.61 ± 0.66 μm (control), 9.46 ± 0.73 μm (0.025% saponin), and 9.49 ± 0.56 μm (0.05% saponin), indicating no significant size alteration due to saponin treatment.', 'Cosine_configuration_description': '', 'process_procedure_description': ''}

{'sample_description': 'The research utilized multiple datasets including a Training dataset composed of 236 CPTAC Study 6 Orbitrap files (2,766,504 spectra) used to train ion intensity variation distributions; Yeast and Human Ion Trap peptide spectral libraries from NIST (May 2011 build) containing 78,825 and 310,688 spectra respectively; a Yeast Test dataset consisting of 9,809 MS/MS spectra from an arbitrarily selected CPTAC Study 6 file excluded from training; and a HeLa S3 Test dataset containing 11,723 MS/MS spectra from human HeLa S3 cells tryptically digested and acquired on an LTQ Orbitrap Classic. All spectra were searched with SpectraST v4.0 using 2 Th m/z tolerance against the NIST Yeast Ion Trap library, filtered to 1% FDR, yielding 396,526 identified spectra from 18,440 unique precursors. Decoy libraries were generated via peptide shuffle and reposition method. Library spectra were preprocessed by annotating peaks with b, y, b++, y++, a ions, their +1 Da 13C isotope peaks, −18 Da H2O losses, and −17 Da NH3 losses; non-annotated and precursor peaks were removed; and all peak intensities were square-root transformed in both library and query spectra.', 'Cosine_configuration_description': 'Cosine similarity was computed as the normalized dot product between two Euclidean-norm-1 vectors: cos(L, SL) = Σi Li × SLi, where L is a library spectrum normalized to ||L|| = 1.0, and SL = NormProj(S, L) is the normalized projection of query spectrum S onto L, defined as Proj(S, L)i = Si if Li > 0, else 0, followed by normalization: SLi = Proj(S, L)i / ||Proj(S, L)||, with ||Proj(S, L)|| = √(Σi Proj(S, L)i²). The cosine score was discretized into nc bins for dynamic programming, and only bins where Li ≠ 0 were considered in projection and log-ratio calculations. For replicate modeling, cosine scores were computed between L and normalized projected replicates RL = NormProj(R, L), and the full SLGF distribution was extracted from LibDP(n, *, 1) — i.e., the dynamic programming table’s final layer restricted to unit Euclidean norm (p = 1). The p-value was defined as ProbL(cos(L, RL) < T), where T is the observed cosine between L and the query’s projected spectrum SL.', 'process_procedure_description': 'The study developed and applied Tremolo, a spectral library search tool implementing the Spectral Library Generating Function (SLGF) framework.

All acquisitions used identical core parameters: TE/TR = 116/3000 ms, FOV = 240 mm, acquisition matrix = 128 × 128, in-plane resolution = 2 mm², slice thickness = 5 mm, no gap, 8 sagittal slices, partial Fourier (half-scan factor = 68.75%), and 15 repetitions per diffusion scheme.'}
{'sample_description': 'The study included ten patients with short QT syndrome (SQTS) from three families: two Italian families (F1 and F2) with documented gain-of-function mutations in the HERG gene, and one Finnish family (F3) with no identified mutation in HERG, KCNQ1, or KCNJ2 but with well-documented life-threatening arrhythmias (e.g., ventricular tachycardia, ventricular fibrillation, aborted sudden cardiac death, or syncope). All SQTS subjects underwent clinical examination, echocardiography, 12-lead electrocardiogram (ECG), and — for symptomatic individuals — programmed electrical stimulation (PES) to assess inducibility of ventricular tachyarrhythmias. The control population consisted of 149 healthy subjects (mean age 51 ± 6 years, 72 males) recruited from Oulu University Hospital and subjected to thorough clinical evaluation, two-dimensional and M-mode echocardiography, and extensive laboratory testing.', 'Cosine_configuration_description': "The software used was a custom-made application (not named 'Cosine' in the text; the term 'TCRT' stands for 'total cosine R-to-T', which is a computed parameter, not a software name) described earlier in reference 7. It automatically calculated T-wave/QRS loop descriptors from digitized 12-lead ECGs. Specifically, it performed principal component analysis (PCA) to construct T-wave and QRS loops in Frank's three-dimensional space using matrix modification and singular value decomposition (SVD). The software rotated each loop so its longest axis aligned with the x-axis; defined the longest axis as 'width' (W) and the perpendicular second-longest axis as 'height' (H); computed the H/W ratio; arranged a bounding rectangle around the loop divided into 100 subrectangles (10 × 10) to compute T-wave loop dispersion (TWLD) as the count of subrectangles traversed by the loop’s borderline; and calculated TCRT as the cosine of the angle between the main vectors (first principal components) of the T-wave and QRS loops in 3D space. No explicit configuration parameters (e.g., filtering settings, sampling rate, noise thresholds, or user-adjustable options) were reported — only the algorithmic steps and geometric definitions.", 'process_procedure_description': 'ECG acquisition: Standard 12-lead ECGs were recorded at 50 mm/s paper speed for the Finnish family and controls, and at 25 mm/s for the Italian families; all subjects were in sinus rhythm. Digitization: ECGs were scanned and digitized using UN-SCAN-IT Graph Digitizing System Version 6.0 (Silk Scientific Inc., Orem, UT). Software processing: A custom-made software application (described in reference 7) automatically computed T-wave/QRS loop descriptors via principal component analysis (PCA) and singular value decomposition (SVD) in Frank’s XYZ lead system. Key outputs included width (W), height (H), H/W ratio, TWLD (based on 10×10 grid traversal), and TCRT (cosine of angle between dominant T-wave and QRS loop vectors). Manual measurements: QT intervals were measured manually using the tangential method; QTc was calculated using Bazett’s formula. Genetic analysis: Genomic DNA was extracted from peripheral blood leukocytes using Puregene kit (Qiagen); exons of HERG, KCNQ1, and KCNJ2 were amplified by PCR, purified with ExoSAP-IT (USB), and sequenced bidirectionally on ABI PRISM 3100-Avant DNA Sequencer (Applied Biosystems). Statistical analysis: Data were analyzed using SPSS 10.1; continuous variables expressed as mean ± SD; group comparisons performed using nonparametric Mann–Whitney U test; significance threshold p < 0.05; abnormality defined as outside the 99% confidence interval of controls.'}
{'sample_description': 'A total of 784 fecal samples were collected from six animal sources: humans (59 samples yielding E. coli, 65 yielding Enterococcus spp.), cows (71 samples), deer (48 samples), dogs (64 samples), chicken (69 samples), and gulls (107 samples).

Analyses were performed using GraphPad Prism v6.07 and Microsoft Excel 2013, with significance set at p < 0.05 for this exploratory study.'}
{'sample_description': 'The study included 56 obese subjects (21 women and 13 men in the OB group; 12 women and 10 men in the ObT2D group) aged 40–60 years, all with BMI ≥ 30 kg/m². The OB group had fasting glucose < 100 mg/dL, while the ObT2D group met diagnostic criteria for type 2 diabetes (fasting glucose ≥ 126 mg/dL and 2-hour OGTT glucose ≥ 200 mg/dL). All subjects were physically inactive for ≥6 months, nonsmokers, free of peripheral neuropathy or overt heart disease (confirmed by physician-supervised maximal exercise stress test), and not taking beta-blockers or medications that alter HR or BP. Premenopausal women were tested during days 1–10 of their menstrual cycle; only 5 postmenopausal women were on hormone therapy. Subjects underwent testing before and after a 16-week supervised aerobic training program. Beat-to-beat R-R interval (RRI) data were acquired continuously at 1000 Hz using a modified CM5-lead ECG interfaced with a Biopac digital acquisition system (Santa Barbara, CA); only artifact-free, ectopic-free 5-minute epochs during paced breathing (12 breaths/min) in supine and upright tilt positions were analyzed. Inclusion required >98% qualified beats (i.e., <2% filtered R-R intervals) after automated and visual editing per Huikuri et al. methodology; for entropy analyses, >200 consecutive stable, ectopic-free RRI data points were used, with identical beat counts applied pre- and post-training within each subject.', 'Spectral entropy_configuration_description': "The study did not use 'Spectral entropy' software; instead, it employed two distinct nonlinear entropy measures: Sample Entropy (SampEn) and Lempel-Ziv Entropy (LZEn), both calculated using WinCPRS software (Absolute Aliens Oy, Turku, Finland). For SampEn: embedding dimension m = 2; tolerance r = 20% of the standard deviation of the RRI time series; computed on >200 consecutive stable, ectopic-free RRI data points; output range 0–2, where higher values indicate greater irregularity. For LZEn: RRI values were binarized relative to the mean (values above mean → '1', below → '0'), forming a binary sequence; LZEn was computed based on the number of insertion-and-copying operations required to reconstruct the sequence using Kolmogorov complexity principles; higher LZEn values reflect greater signal randomness; same >200-beat dataset as SampEn was used. Neither SampEn nor LZEn is spectral entropy — spectral entropy would involve frequency-domain decomposition (e.g., FFT or autoregressive power spectra), whereas these are time-domain pattern-based entropies.", 'process_procedure_description': 'HR complexity analysis was performed using WinCPRS software (Absolute Aliens Oy, Turku, Finland). The procedure involved: (1) acquiring continuous ECG at 1000 Hz via Biopac system; (2) extracting R-R intervals; (3) applying rigorous artifact and ectopic beat removal (automatic editing + visual inspection) following Huikuri et al. criteria (R-R intervals deviating >30% from prior interval flagged as premature and replaced by local neighborhood average); (4) selecting only 5-min epochs during paced breathing (12 breaths/min) in supine and upright tilt positions; (5) ensuring >98% qualified beats per epoch; (6) extracting >200 consecutive stable, ectopic-free RRI points for entropy computation; (7) computing SampEn with m = 2 and r = 0.2 × SD(RRI); (8) computing LZEn on the same RRI segment after binarization about the mean; (9) maintaining identical beat count across pre- and post-training comparisons within each subject. No other software (e.g., Heart Signal, Cosmed Quark b2, Portapres, SPSS) was used for entropy calculations — WinCPRS was exclusively used for both SampEn and LZEn.'}

{'sample_description': "The study utilized two primary spectral datasets: (1) a million-scale in-silico electron ionization mass spectrometry (EI-MS) library, generated as described by Yang et al.

No other software (e.g., Heart Signal, Cosmed Quark b2, Portapres, SPSS) was used for entropy calculations — WinCPRS was exclusively used for both SampEn and LZEn.'}

{'sample_description': "The study utilized two primary spectral datasets: (1) a million-scale in-silico electron ionization mass spectrometry (EI-MS) library, generated as described by Yang et al. and used for training and fine-tuning; from this, a subset of 50,000 spectra was randomly selected for the Tanimoto similarity-guided fine-tuning stage due to computational constraints in pairwise Tanimoto score calculation; and (2) the NIST 2023 Tandem Mass Spectral Library (mainlib), from which query spectra were selected exclusively for the held-out test set — ensuring complete separation from the in-silico training data. All spectra underwent peak filtering to retain only the top 30 most intense peaks per spectrum, based on intensity-based selection to enrich structurally informative signals while removing low-intensity background noise. The textualization pipeline converted each filtered spectrum into a structured text format with explicit semantic labels: 'base_peak' (peak with maximum intensity), 'max_peak' (highest m/z among top 30), 'key_peaks' (top 3 most intense peaks excluding base_peak), and 'extra_peaks' (remaining peaks among the top 30). This textual representation served as input to the LLM-based models.", 'Spec2Vec_configuration_description': "The text does not describe any configuration, parameters, or usage of Spec2Vec beyond mentioning it as one of the baseline similarity algorithms (alongside WCS and CS) evaluated in the 'Two strategies in applying the base peak matching heuristic' subsection. No details are provided regarding Spec2Vec's implementation, version, embedding dimensionality, preprocessing steps, cosine similarity settings, spectral binning, peak tolerance, or any hyperparameters (e.g., min_intensity, max_mz, n_neighbors, vector_size, window_size, epochs). Its use is purely comparative and contextual within the base peak heuristic evaluation, with no methodological specification given.", 'process_procedure_description': "The research employed a multi-stage software-driven pipeline to develop and evaluate the LLM4MS model: (1) Mass spectra preprocessing and textualization: Raw spectra from the in-silico EI-MS library were filtered to retain only the top k=30 most intense peaks; each spectrum was then converted into a structured textual format with labeled peak types ('base_peak', 'max_peak', 'key_peaks', 'extra_peaks') to encode domain-specific spectral semantics. (2) Model development: Starting from the publicly available Llama 3.1-8B (8-billion-parameter) base LLM, the pipeline applied unsupervised pre-adaptation using the LLM2Vec framework — specifically Masked Next Token Prediction (MNTP) and Simple Contrastive Learning of Sentence Embeddings (SimCSE) — on general-domain corpora (e.g., English Wikipedia) to produce the intermediate LLM2Vec model. This was followed by domain-specific contrastive fine-tuning on the 50,000 textualized spectra, guided by pairwise Tanimoto similarity scores computed from the original spectral data: for each anchor spectrum, the highest-Tanimoto-scoring spectrum was treated as the positive pair, and others as negative pairs, optimizing embeddings via a contrastive learning objective. (3) Similarity computation: After embedding generation using LLM4MS, cosine similarity was computed between n=4096-dimensional embedding vectors A and B as S_C(A,B) = (A ⋅ B) / (∥A∥ ∥B∥), where ∥·∥ denotes the Euclidean (L2) norm; this metric was chosen for its invariance to vector magnitude and established efficacy in semantic similarity tasks. (4) Baseline comparison: GPT-4o embeddings were obtained via its official API using identical textualized spectra; performance was evaluated using Recall@1 on the NIST23 test set. (5) Hardware and environment: All training and fine-tuning procedures were conducted on a Linux machine equipped with an AMD 128-core CPU (2 GHz/core), 1 TB RAM, and one RTX A6000 GPU (48 GB VRAM); the final LLM4MS model and GUI software are publicly available on Zenodo under a noncommercial use license."}
{'sample_description': 'The research used multiple public mass spectral libraries for training and benchmarking: the GNPS library, the MassBank EU library, the MassBank of North America (MoNA) library, and MS2 spectra from MSnLib (created by Brungs et al.).

Statistical analyses were conducted in Python 3.10.9 using SciPy 1.13.0: two-way ANOVA (factors: pulse level and carrier level) on EOG area-under-curve and ERP peak latency/amplitude in 85–115 ms window; inhibition percentage computed as [1 − (GP/PO)] for each ISI (0, 60, 120, 240 ms); post hoc Tukey-corrected multiple comparisons; significance threshold p < 0.05; assumptions verified prior to testing.'}
{'sample_description': 'The study involved seventeen volunteers with a mean age of 30 ± 4.29 years (6 women, 11 men), including participants from medical and non-medical backgrounds (e.g., engineers, pilots, physicians). None reported vision impairment. Twelve participants had no prior experience with eye surgery tasks, four had prior simulator experience, and one had assisted in real eye surgery. All participants underwent ethical approval from the Ethics Committee of the Technical University of Munich, provided informed consent, and completed a controlled VR-based user study involving two surgical simulation modes (Fundus and iOCT) using a VR HMD and haptic devices.', 'BLINK_configuration_description': '', 'process_procedure_description': 'The study employed a within-subject, counterbalanced experimental design where each participant completed 15 trials per mode (Mode 1: Fundus — conventional microscope view; Mode 2: iOCT — microscope view augmented with simulated intraoperative optical coherence tomography B-scans). Physiological data were collected using Varjo Aero VR HMD with integrated 80 Hz binocular eye tracking (for blink rate and pupil diameter) and BiosignalsPlux wireless ECG (1000 Hz sampling) for heart rate (HR) and heart rate variability (HRV, quantified as RMSSD). Data preprocessing included: (1) blink rate calculation as number of eye openness state changes per minute divided by two; (2) pupil diameter preprocessing to remove invalid samples due to blinks or tracking loss, followed by averaging over 200 ms windows and generation of a third time series from binocular fusion; (3) subtractive baseline correction using mean values from two-minute nature-video rest phases before each task; (4) HRV computation via HeartPy’s process_segmentwise function using 30-second segments; (5) temporal alignment of self-reported mental effort (collected every third trial on a 0–20 scale) via linear interpolation; (6) outlier removal using the interquartile range method; and (7) statistical analysis using paired samples t-tests after confirming normality via Shapiro-Wilk test. The entire procedure included eye calibration, pre-task rest (2 min), training phase (un-timed familiarization), testing phase (15 difficulty-increasing trials with target-hold duration increasing from 2 to 4 seconds), post-task rest (2 min), and post-session NASA-TLX + qualitative questionnaire — repeated for both modes in randomized order.'}


{'sample_description': "The study utilized two main sample types: (1) K562 (ATCC CCL-243) human leukemia cell line for CTCF MNase HiChIP experiments, where four biological replicates were generated — two 'YET' samples digested with 1 µL MNase and two 'GW' samples digested with 2 µL MNase — each starting from 5 million crosslinked cells (3 mM DSG + 1% formaldehyde), followed by chromatin immunoprecipitation using 500 ng of anti-CTCF antibody (Cell Signaling, cat# 3418) and 1500 ng of chromatin (40–70% mononucleosomes); and (2) mouse embryonic stem cells (mESC; RRID: CVCL_J962) for RCMC (Restriction-Cut and Multiplexed Capture) experiments, including three conditions — wild-type (WT), 3-hour DMSO-treated (control), and 3-hour IAA-treated (cohesin degradation) — with two biological replicates per condition, focusing on three captured genomic loci (Fbn2 on chr18:57,899,000–58,901,000, Ppm1g on chr5:31,100,000–32,225,000, and Klf1 on chr8:84,120,000–85,130,000) in mm10 reference genome coordinates. All libraries were sequenced as 150 bp paired-end reads on Illumina NextSeq 2000, yielding ~172 million reads per K562 replicate and re-sequenced mESC RCMC libraries at comparable depth.

Crystal structure visualizations were created using Mercury (version not specified). Microcrystal size measurements and mask generation for diffracted X-ray blinking (DXB) analysis were conducted using ImageJ (version not specified). DXB analysis and all subsequent data treatment were carried out using custom Python scripts. During DXB analysis, pixels that failed to retain ≥90% of their initial signal intensity were discarded. After computing the autocorrelation function (ACF), curve fitting was performed using non-linear least squares as implemented in SciPy’s curve_fit function; only fits with all positive coefficients were retained. Statistical significance testing employed the nonparametric Wilcoxon rank-sum test (Mann–Whitney U test), also via SciPy. All software versions are unspecified in the text, and no configuration files or parameter files (e.g., BLINK-specific settings) are described.'}
{'sample_description': 'The study enrolled 55 medical staff (MS) — including physicians and nurses — from the First Hospital of Jilin University, China, aged 23 to 59 years (mean age 35.72 ± 10.10), with 10 males and 45 females. A total of 110 eyes were included (both eyes of each participant). Participants were recruited randomly and voluntarily. Exclusion criteria included autoimmune systemic diseases, ocular diseases other than cataract, and history of ocular surgery. Ethical approval was obtained from the Ethics Committee at the First Hospital of Jilin University (approval number: 22K017-001), and written informed consent was obtained from all participants. Sample size calculation was performed using PASS.15 software, based on a lipid layer thickness (LLT) difference of 1.4 ± 4 SD between groups, targeting an effect size (d) of 0.35, significance level (α) of 0.05, statistical power (1−β) of 0.8, and a two-tailed test — resulting in a required sample size of 67 participants per group; however, the actual enrollment was 55 participants (110 eyes), likely reflecting feasibility constraints or refined assumptions.', 'BLINK_configuration_description': '', 'process_procedure_description': 'The study employed the Gaush iDea Dry Eye Diagnostic Device for objective ocular surface measurements. The device uses Dr. Guillon’s dynamic classification method (Zang et al., 2018) to analyze blink dynamics and quantify lipid layer thickness (LLT). Specifically, the software captures and analyzes all frames from full eye opening to complete closure during blinking, computes the average thickness across all frames, and generates an averaged LLT result. LLT was measured in four quadrants (superior, inferior, nasal, temporal) by rotating a magnetic cone; the algorithm incorporates multiple components including area-of-interest definition, color adjustment, and fringe-type analysis. Since the lipid layer is oily and deflects light according to its thickness, the camera acquires interference patterns (fringes), and the software calculates LLT based on observed color spectra (as validated by Dr. Guillon’s methodology). Tear meniscus height (TMH) was measured as the average of ≥3 readings taken at different positions along the tear river. Non-invasive tear break-up time (NIBUT), meibomian gland (MG) dropout, blink pattern (including partial blink rate, PBR), and lid margin abnormalities (assessed via slit-lamp microscopy and scored 0–4) were also recorded. All measurements were performed twice per participant — before and after >4 hours of clinical work — by the same physician. Subjective assessment included the Ocular Surface Disease Index (OSDI) questionnaire, scored using the formula: OSDI = (total score × 25) / number of questions answered, with severity stratified as normal (<13), mild (13–22), moderate (23–32), and severe (>33). Daily visual display terminal (VDT) usage time (VDTt) over the prior 3 months was collected by summing self-reported daily hours of mobile phone, television, computer, and other VDT use. Statistical analyses were conducted using SPSS® for Windows (version 22.0); data are presented as mean ± standard deviation; parametric tests (t-tests, ANOVA) or non-parametric tests (Wilcoxon signed-rank, rank-sum) were applied based on data distribution; Spearman’s correlation coefficient assessed associations among parameters; significance threshold was p < 0.05.'}
{'sample_description': 'The study involved 12 healthy volunteers (5 females and 7 males) aged 34.3 ± 12.6 years (range: 26–57 years).

Daily visual display terminal (VDT) usage time (VDTt) over the prior 3 months was collected by summing self-reported daily hours of mobile phone, television, computer, and other VDT use. Statistical analyses were conducted using SPSS® for Windows (version 22.0); data are presented as mean ± standard deviation; parametric tests (t-tests, ANOVA) or non-parametric tests (Wilcoxon signed-rank, rank-sum) were applied based on data distribution; Spearman’s correlation coefficient assessed associations among parameters; significance threshold was p < 0.05.'}
{'sample_description': 'The study involved 12 healthy volunteers (5 females and 7 males) aged 34.3 ± 12.6 years (range: 26–57 years). All participants were free from neurological or mental disorders and substance abuse in the past 2 years, were non-smokers, and did not use hormone agents including contraceptives. Written informed consent was obtained from all subjects, and the study was approved by the Ethics Committee of Aichi Developmental Disability Center (approval number: R04-09) and conducted in accordance with the Declaration of Helsinki.', 'BLINK_configuration_description': '', 'process_procedure_description': 'Electrical stimulation was delivered to the right supraorbital nerve (SON) using square-wave pulses (0.5 ms duration) via two disposable Ag/AgCl gel electrodes (10 mm diameter; Biorode SDC-H, Vyaire Medical, Tokyo), placed near the supraorbital foramen and ~3 cm above it. Stimulus intensity was set at 1.5× R1 threshold for test stimuli and 0.9× R1 threshold for prepulses; R1 threshold was defined as the current eliciting R1 in 50% of stimulations. Stimulus frequency was 2 Hz. Blink reflexes were recorded from the right orbicularis oculi muscle using a single-axis accelerometer (MPS110, Medi Sens Inc., Tokyo; 8 × 8 × 4 mm) placed on the central lower eyelid. Signals were filtered (1–250 Hz analog filter), amplified, and acquired using an EMG/EP measuring system (MEB-2300, Nihon Kohden, Tokyo) at 10,000 Hz sampling rate, with an analysis window from 40 ms pre-stimulus to 160 ms post-stimulus onset. The PPI paradigm used prepulse–test intervals (PTIs) of 5 ms and 10–150 ms in 10-ms steps, organized into four randomized blocks: Block 1 (5, 10, 20, 30 ms), Block 2 (40, 50, 60, 70 ms), Block 3 (80, 90, 100, 110 ms), and Block 4 (120, 130, 140, 150 ms); each block included five or six conditions (test alone, prepulse alone, and prepulse + test at four PTIs). Each condition had 15 epochs per run, with either 10 or 12 runs per block (totaling 30 epochs per condition per block), separated by 30-s inter-run intervals and 2-min inter-block intervals. Run order within each block followed an ascending–descending (U-shaped) sequence, with test-alone runs positioned first and last. For analysis, blink responses were fully rectified and averaged per block; baseline (pre-stimulus) was subtracted, and area under the curve (AUC) between 15–40 ms was calculated as R1 response magnitude. Inhibition was quantified as %Amplitude = (R1 amplitude for prepulse + test / R1 amplitude for test alone) × 100. Statistical analyses included one-way repeated-measures ANOVA across 16 PTIs, 95% confidence interval assessment for significance (non-overlap with 100% indicating significant inhibition), two-way ANOVA (sex × PTI), simple and partial correlation (age vs. PPI, controlling for sex), and one-way repeated-measures ANOVA for block-order effects on test-alone amplitude. Significance threshold was p < 0.05. All statistical analyses were performed using SPSS version 24.'}
{'sample_description': 'Three male human volunteers with unilateral, upper limb loss (one right arm and two left arm) participated in the study. Subject 1 had a right trans-radial amputation (2004) and was implanted in 2012 with three 8-contact flat interface nerve electrodes (FINEs) around the median, ulnar, and radial nerves.

Statistical analyses included one-way repeated-measures ANOVA across 16 PTIs, 95% confidence interval assessment for significance (non-overlap with 100% indicating significant inhibition), two-way ANOVA (sex × PTI), simple and partial correlation (age vs. PPI, controlling for sex), and one-way repeated-measures ANOVA for block-order effects on test-alone amplitude. Significance threshold was p < 0.05. All statistical analyses were performed using SPSS version 24.'}
{'sample_description': 'Three male human volunteers with unilateral, upper limb loss (one right arm and two left arm) participated in the study. Subject 1 had a right trans-radial amputation (2004) and was implanted in 2012 with three 8-contact flat interface nerve electrodes (FINEs) around the median, ulnar, and radial nerves. Subjects 2 and 3 had left trans-radial amputations (2013 and 2012, respectively) and were implanted in 2016 and 2017 with two 16-contact curved FINEs (CFINEs) around the median and ulnar nerves. Subjects 2 and 3 also had implanted EMG electrode pairs in eight forearm muscles. For all tests, only the median nerve cuff was used. All subjects underwent repeated test sessions over a 2-year period (1–2 h per session, every 2–6 weeks), with varying numbers of sessions per experiment: Subject 1 completed 6 simple reaction time (SRT), 3 intensity-based SRT, and 9 backmasking sessions; Subject 2 completed 5 SRT and 3 backmasking sessions; Subject 3 completed 11 SRT, 6 intensity-based SRT, and 17 backmasking sessions. All procedures were approved by the U.S. FDA Investigational Device Exemption, Cleveland VA Medical Center IRB, and Department of the Navy Human Research Protection Program; informed consent was obtained from all subjects.', 'BLINK_configuration_description': '', 'process_procedure_description': "The study employed three experimental paradigms: (1) Simple Reaction Time (SRT) Test: Subjects contracted forearm muscles upon perceiving visual (LEDs), tactile (peripheral nerve stimulation via FINE/CFINE or vibrotactile motor), or combined visual-tactile stimuli. Stimuli were randomized across 50 trials per modality, grouped into three blocks with breaks; reaction time was measured as the inflection point from baseline to first major peak in filtered, rectified, and smoothed EMG (recorded via surface EMG for Subject 1 and implanted EMG for Subjects 2 and 3). (2) Intensity-Based Reaction Time Test: Stimulation intensity was modulated by varying pulse width (from threshold to 'max comfortable') while holding pulse amplitude and frequency constant; intensity was quantified using Activation Charge Rate (ACR = PF × PA × (PW − PW_thresh)), and reaction times were fit to Piéron’s law (SRT = β(PW − PW_thresh)^−α) using least-squares fitting in Python. Ten equally spaced pulse widths were tested across thumb and index fingertip percepts, with five blocks of 100 randomized trials (five per intensity level). (3) Backmasking Experiment: To assess pre-perceptual processing, three trial types were used: (i) weak 50-ms stimulus (100% perceptible alone), (ii) strong 50-ms 'comfortable' stimulus delayed by 150 ms, and (iii) backmasked trial with weak stimulus followed by strong stimulus after 100-ms interburst interval. Subjects reported number of perceived bursts and reacted as fast as possible; reaction time for masked trials was measured from the strong stimulus onset if only one burst was reported. Catch trials (two strong stimuli) confirmed masking efficacy. Statistical analysis included ANOVA with Tukey correction (SRT), least-squares curve fitting in Python (intensity-based), and t-tests (Subjects 1 & 3) or Mann–Whitney U tests (Subject 2) for backmasking comparisons."}

{'sample_description': 'The study involved human subjects undergoing magnetoencephalography (MEG) recordings while simultaneously using MEG-compatible eye-tracking devices. Subjects were required to have perfect or corrected-to-perfect vision; soft contact lenses were recommended over eyeglasses due to ferromagnetic interference risks and comfort within the fixed MEG sensor helmet. Eye makeup (e.g., mascara) was discouraged as it could introduce MEG artifacts and impair pupil detection in video-oculography. Ferromagnetic materials—including jewelry, implants, piercings, tattoos, and standard eyeglass frames—were strictly prohibited.

Catch trials (two strong stimuli) confirmed masking efficacy. Statistical analysis included ANOVA with Tukey correction (SRT), least-squares curve fitting in Python (intensity-based), and t-tests (Subjects 1 & 3) or Mann–Whitney U tests (Subject 2) for backmasking comparisons."}

{'sample_description': 'The study involved human subjects undergoing magnetoencephalography (MEG) recordings while simultaneously using MEG-compatible eye-tracking devices. Subjects were required to have perfect or corrected-to-perfect vision; soft contact lenses were recommended over eyeglasses due to ferromagnetic interference risks and comfort within the fixed MEG sensor helmet. Eye makeup (e.g., mascara) was discouraged as it could introduce MEG artifacts and impair pupil detection in video-oculography. Ferromagnetic materials—including jewelry, implants, piercings, tattoos, and standard eyeglass frames—were strictly prohibited. Subject positioning followed MEG safety and signal quality guidelines: subjects sat upright with head stabilized inside the Vectorview 306-channel MEG helmet, and visual stimuli were presented via back-projection screen with careful attention to viewing angle (smaller angles preferred to minimize saccades and improve eye-tracking accuracy). Calibration procedures varied depending on use case: for task monitoring only, fewer calibration points sufficed; for gaze-contingent stimulus presentation or MEG data rejection based on precise gaze position, full calibration across the stimulus area was required.', 'BLINK_configuration_description': '', 'process_procedure_description': 'The research employed a multimodal neuroimaging methodology integrating MEG and eye tracking. MEG data were acquired using a 306-channel Neuromag Vectorview system (102 sensor units, each with two planar gradiometers and one magnetometer), housed first at the Brain Research Unit of Helsinki University of Technology and later at Aalto NeuroImaging Infrastructure, both within magnetically shielded rooms (Euroshield and Imedco AG). Two MEG-compatible eye trackers were used sequentially: the iView X MEye Tracker (SMI, 50 Hz sampling rate, 2006–2010) and the EyeLink 1000 (SR-Research, 2000 Hz sampling rate, <1.4 ms latency, used thereafter); the TRACKPixx3 (VPixx Technologies, 2 kHz binocular video-oculography) was also referenced as an alternative. Eye tracking utilized infrared (IR) video-oculography with dark-pupil/cornea-reflection (Purkinje image) detection. Hardware setup prioritized electromagnetic compatibility: IR illuminators and cameras were placed ~1 m from the MEG helmet to avoid interference (empirically validated—no systematic noise beyond 60 cm); nonmagnetic materials (wooden table with brass mechanics) were used for mounting; ferrite beads were applied to power cables at feed-throughs between the magnetically shielded room (MSR) and stimulus cabinet to suppress high-frequency noise; and optical fiber video output (EyeLink) was used to eliminate conductive/antenna effects. Synchronization between MEG and eye tracker was achieved via multiple methods: (1) TTL triggers sent simultaneously from a Stimulus PC to both systems at trial onset/offset; (2) direct digital TTL outputs from the EyeLink Host PC (via DT334 PCI analog/digital card and custom breakout box with BNC connectors) for time-critical gaze-contingent events (e.g., fixation-triggered stimulus presentation); and (3) analog output of gaze x/y coordinates and pupil diameter fed into MEG misc channels for continuous, hardware-synchronized recording. Eye-tracking data were used for multiple purposes: (a) subject monitoring (real-time infrared face view); (b) online/offline MEG artifact rejection (blinks/saccades detected via velocity-based algorithms per Stampe, 1993, with latency compensation); (c) task compliance monitoring (e.g., fixation adherence); (d) gaze-contingent stimulus presentation (e.g., Ramkumar et al., 2013; Pan et al., 2021/2022); (e) trial labeling and averaging based on gaze behavior (e.g., Hirvenkari et al., 2010); and (f) interactive paradigms where gaze served as a pointing tool (e.g., Kauppi et al., 2015), with selections confirmed via button press to avoid Midas touch errors. All procedures adhered to MEG best practices for minimizing electromagnetic interference, optimizing signal fidelity, and ensuring subject compatibility.'}
{'sample_description': "Four MNase HiChIP libraries were generated from K562 human leukemia cell line using the Cantata Bio / Dovetail Genomics MNase HiChIP kit.

Each stimulus subtended 12.6° × 20° visual angle. Participants viewed stimuli from 50 cm distance, stabilized by a chinrest, and responded via keyboard ('left arrow'/'right arrow') to indicate target (asterisk, 2.5° × 2.5°) location (left/right of face) as quickly and accurately as possible. Trials began with a randomized fixation interval (900–1000 ms), followed by a 500-ms neutral straight-gaze face, then a 100-ms gaze-shift cue (left/straight/right), followed by emotional expression (neutral or fear) presented for 0, 100, 200, or 400 ms, and finally the target. The inter-trial interval was 1000 ms blank screen. The experiment comprised one practice block (24 trials) and five experimental blocks (each containing 128 trials per 16 main conditions — 2 expressions × 2 congruency levels [congruent/incongruent] × 4 durations — plus 32 straight-gaze trials), totaling ~60 minutes. Eye movements were monitored via EOG recorded at 1000 Hz using AcqKnowledge 4.1 (Biopac Systems) with electrodes placed bilaterally on temples (horizontal), above/below right eye (vertical), and forehead (reference); baseline EOG was recorded for 5 s before the experiment, and trials exceeding baseline amplitude in horizontal or vertical EOG were classified as saccade or blink trials and later synchronized with RT data for analysis. Statistical analyses were performed using SPSS Statistics, including repeated-measures ANOVAs (2 × 2 × 4 design) on correct reaction times and gaze cueing effect (GCE = RT_incongruent − RT_congruent), with Greenhouse–Geisser correction applied where sphericity was violated; significance threshold was p < 0.05, and post-hoc comparisons used Bonferroni correction with adjusted alpha = 0.008."}


{'sample_description': 'The study used filamentous fungal strains, specifically Aspergillus nidulans, which were genetically modified to express the genetically-encoded calcium biosensor R-GECO for Ca2+ imaging, and some strains were tagged with GFP as described in Supplemental Methods and Table S4. Fungal cultures were grown on minimal medium agar plates at 30°C for 2 days; colony edges were excised into 5 mm squares and placed in glass dishes for live-cell microscopy. Strains and media details are listed in Table S3.', 'BLINK_configuration_description': '', 'process_procedure_description': 'Ca2+ imaging was performed using a motorized fluorescence stereo microscope (SMZ-25; Nikon) equipped with a P2-SHR PLAN APO 1x/0.16 objective lens and an sCMOS camera (ORCA-Flash4.0 V2; Hamamatsu Photonics). R-GECO was excited using a mercury lamp (Intensilight Hg Illuminator; Nikon), a 561/14-nm excitation filter (FF01-561/14-25, Semrock), and a 575-nm dichroic mirror (Di02-R561-25 × 36; Semrock); emission was collected through a 609/54-nm filter (FF01-609/54-25; Semrock) and acquired using NIS-Elements imaging software (Nikon). For localized heat shock experiments, hyphae were observed under an inverted microscope IX-83 (Olympus), and point laser irradiation was applied using an IR-LEGO 1000 system (Sigma Koki) equipped with custom-made UPlanSApo 20x/0.75 and UPlanSApo 40x/0.95 objective lenses (Olympus), operating at 15 mW for 5–20 s. Image analysis was conducted using MetaMorph and ImageJ software. GFP-trap immunoprecipitation followed by liquid chromatography-tandem mass spectrometry (LC-MS/MS) was performed for protein identification; proteomic data are deposited in ProteomeXchange (PXD027777) and jPOSTrepo (JPST001285). All other experimental methods are detailed in the Supplementary Information.'}

{'sample_description': "The study involved 26 trained readers (19 board-certified consultant radiologists, 4 consultant radiographers, and 3 advanced practitioners) from the UK NHS Breast Screening Programme (NHSBSP), recruited between November 2019 and February 2021 as part of the PROSPECTS trial (ClinicalTrials.gov ID: NCT03733106). All participants provided written informed consent and were approved under London-Dulwich Research Ethics Committee oversight.

All participants provided written informed consent, and the study was approved by the Comitato Etico Regionale Liguria (prot. n∘ 452REG2015 - 107-17/12/18) in accordance with the 2013 Declaration of Helsinki.', 'BLINK_configuration_description': '', 'process_procedure_description': "The research employed a machine learning–based classification pipeline implemented in Python 3.7.1 using Pandas libraries. Two distinct datasets were constructed: (1) a TBR dataset containing 16 features per participant—area under the curve (AUC, mV×ms), latency (ms), and duration (ms) of bilateral R2 components (from both eyes and both sides of supraorbital nerve stimulation), plus latency and duration of the early ipsilateral R1 component; and (2) an HBR dataset containing 72 features per participant—AUC, latency, and duration of HBR waveforms recorded bilaterally from both eyes, elicited from both wrists, under static and voluntary movement conditions (Up-movement and Down-movement), across two target elbow positions relative to the face (NEAR: 10° beyond maximal flexion; FAR: 10° short of maximal extension). No missing values were present. Feature extraction was performed on EMG signals (recorded at 1 kHz, bandpass filtered 5–5000 Hz, rectified, averaged per condition), with TBR comprising 20 trials (10 per side, bilateral) and HBR comprising 40 trials (10 per side × 2 positions × 2 movement directions). Classification used AdaBoost with Decision Tree weak learners; hyperparameter optimization (number of estimators: 10–70; learning rate: 10⁻³ to 0.7; algorithm: SAMME/SAMME.R) was conducted via Nested 5-Fold Cross-Validation—outer loop (5 folds) for unbiased performance estimation, inner loop (4 folds) for grid-search-based hyperparameter selection using validation-set accuracy. Final model evaluation reported Accuracy, Recall, Precision, and F1-Score per class, computed both as averages over 5 test folds and as 'absolute' metrics from a single confusion matrix aggregating all 5-fold predictions. Feature importance was impurity-based (summing to 1 across all N features), enabling intrinsic feature selection."}


{'sample_description': 'The study used tear samples collected from 92 eligible participants aged 20 years or older working in a shipyard industry, enrolled during their 2020 annual health checkup. Tear samples were collected using sterile plastic capillary tubes (to avoid injury and contamination) via capillary action, yielding approximately 10 µL per sample. Samples were extracted from capillaries by centrifugation. The trace elements measured included vanadium (V), chromium (Cr), manganese (Mn), iron (Fe), cobalt (Co), nickel (Ni), copper (Cu), zinc (Zn), arsenic (As), selenium (Se), rhodium (Rh), cadmium (Cd), mercury (Hg), and lead (Pb). Sample preparation involved mixing with concentrated nitric acid (Fisher Scientific, U.K.), and analytical quality control included deionized water blanks to detect contamination and a certified rock standard (BCR1) solution to verify procedural accuracy.', 'BLINK_configuration_description': '', 'process_procedure_description': 'Trace element concentrations in tear samples were quantified using Inductively Coupled Plasma Mass Spectroscopy (ICP-MS) on a Thermo Fisher iCAP RQ ICP-MS instrument (U.S.A.), following a previously published method. Statistical analyses were performed using SPSS version 22.0 (SPSS Inc., Chicago, IL, U.S.A.). Pairwise correlations between tear trace elements and dry eye parameters were visualized using heatmap illustrations. Weighted Quantile Sum (WQS) regression was applied to identify the highest-contributing trace element for each dry eye parameter. Linear regression models assessed associations between individual trace elements (especially Pb) and dry eye parameters, adjusting for covariates including age, white blood cell count, creatinine, alanine aminotransferase (ALT), and smoking history. Significance was defined as p < 0.05.'}
{'sample_description': 'The study sample consisted of Medicare beneficiaries aged 65 years or older as of January 1, 2019, drawn from two sources: (1) Medicare Advantage (MA) beneficiaries enrolled in plans offered by a large national insurer, and (2) Traditional Medicare (TM) beneficiaries identified from a random 5% national sample with Part A and B coverage.

Fixation agreement across algorithms was quantified using Hooge et al. (2018)’s event-based F1-score, computed before and after merging and selection.'}
{'sample_description': 'A total of 118 participants completed the study (mean age: 14.9 ± 3.8 years; 55.1% female). Adults aged ≥18 years provided written consent; children aged <18 years gave verbal assent and their parents/legal guardians provided written informed consent. All participants were free of neurologic, psychiatric, or ocular diagnoses, were not taking psychotropic medications, and had normal or corrected-to-normal vision. Data from three participants in the random task and four in the metronome task were excluded due to poor eye-tracking quality, resulting in final analyses on 115 participants in the random task and 114 in the metronome task. Eye-tracking data were collected using two EyeLink systems: EyeLink 1000 Plus (n = 83) and EyeLink II (n = 35), both operating at 500 Hz sampling rate with mean accuracy ≤0.5°; multivariate tests confirmed no significant differences between the two systems (all p > 0.19), so data were collapsed for analysis.', 'BLINK_configuration_description': '', 'process_procedure_description': 'Offline eye-tracking analysis was performed using MATLAB R2019b (MathWorks). Saccades were identified based on instantaneous velocity computed from a three-point moving average of x- and y-eye positions (in degrees); a saccade was labeled when velocity exceeded 2 standard deviations above the mean fixation velocity (<50°/s) for at least five consecutive points. Saccade reaction time (SRT) was defined as the time from peripheral target appearance to primary saccade onset. Saccades were classified as predictive (SRT < 90 ms), express (90–120 ms), or regular (>120 ms) based on established neurophysiological criteria and validated by bimodality coefficient analysis (BC = 0.63 for metronome, 0.54 for random task) of SRT distributions >90 ms. For saccade metrics, peak velocity and amplitude were analyzed; saccades with peak velocity >1000°/s were excluded (0.23% of total saccades). The main sequence was modeled using a square-root function (y = V√x) per saccade type (predictive, express, regular), fitted at both group and individual subject levels. Pupil size was analyzed in 2-ms bins, averaged over a 200-ms epoch following saccade end (provided ≥200-ms post-saccadic fixation was maintained), excluding trials with blinks, saccades, fixation onset delays >100 ms, or pupil velocity outside −5000 to 5000°/s. Blink metrics were extracted by detecting loss of eye-tracking signal consistent with literature-defined blink durations (50–300 ms); blink rate was computed as blinks per minute per trial, and blink timing relative to target appearance was analyzed in three epochs (−1000–1000 ms, −1000–0 ms, 0–+1000 ms), further stratified by saccade type. Statistical modeling employed Generalized Additive Models (GAMs) implemented via the mgcv package in R (Wood, 2009), with age modeled as a smooth function (sλ(Agei)) using restricted maximum likelihood (REML) smoothing parameter estimation (k = 9); 95% confidence intervals for developmental change were derived via posterior simulation (1000 draws from multivariate normal distribution of fitted parameters), and first derivatives were used to identify statistically significant periods of change (p < 0.05) and maturation points. Normality was assessed using Kolmogorov–Smirnov test; parametric (e.g., ANOVA, t-tests) or nonparametric tests were applied accordingly, with Bonferroni correction for multiple comparisons.'}
{'sample_description': 'The study used two types of biological samples: (1) free-ranging wild harbour porpoises (Phocoena phocoena, n = 6) captured incidentally in pound nets in inner Danish waters (2012–2018), and (2) free-ranging Blainville’s beaked whales (Mesoplodon densirostris, n = 8) off El Hierro, Canary Islands (2004–2013). Additionally, two captive female harbour porpoises (Sif and Freja, aged 14 and 20 years, respectively; body lengths 1.6 m and 1.58 m) housed at Fjord & Bælt, Denmark, were used in controlled experiments.

{'sample_description': 'ALP (alkaline phosphatase) from bovine intestinal mucosa was purchased from Sigma-Aldrich (Cat # P7923), further purified in house, and fluorescently labeled using amine-NHS chemistry.', 'BLINK_configuration_description': '', 'process_procedure_description': 'ABEL trap-based single-molecule diffusometry was implemented as previously described in reference 26; high-speed single-particle tracking (SPT) was carried out according to the recent protocol in reference 36. The methods are detailed in the SI Appendix, Materials and Methods, but no software named BLINK or its configuration, parameters, or settings are mentioned in the provided text.'}
{'sample_description': 'The study included 30 male military pilots (mean age = 44.23 ± 4.07 years; mean flight time = 3402.10 ± 1196.12 hours; aircraft types: fighters 63.3%, trainers 20%, bombers 10%, airfreighters 6.7%) and 29 male control participants (mean age = 44.07 ± 2.93 years), who were administrative staff members of the Air Force. All participants had normal or corrected-to-normal visual acuity, no color vision deficiencies or color blindness, and no history of major physical diseases or mental disorders. Participants were seated approximately 50 cm from the computer screen during testing.', 'BLINK_configuration_description': '', 'process_procedure_description': "The study employed the classic rapid serial visual presentation (RSVP) paradigm implemented using E-Prime Studio 3.0 software on 14-inch, 1920 × 1080 HD LED ThinkPad monitors with a 60 Hz refresh rate. Stimuli consisted of white numbers (excluding 0, 1, I, O, Q, Z to avoid interference) and two target letters per trial, all displayed in Courier New font, size 40, on a black background. Each trial began with a 1000 ms fixation cross ('+'), followed by a stream of 10 stimuli presented at 1 stimulus per 90 ms (15 ms stimulus duration + 75 ms inter-stimulus interval). The sequence included 4–6 distractor numbers before the first target letter (T1), variable numbers (0–7) of distractors between T1 and the second target letter (T2), and 4–6 distractors after T2. T2 positions relative to T1 defined lags: lag1 (T2 immediately after T1, 90 ms SOA), lag2 (180 ms), up to lag8 (720 ms). The experiment comprised two blocks: a practice block of 8 trials and a formal block of 72 trials. Participants responded by typing the two detected target letters in order immediately after each trial. Attentional Blink (AB) was quantified as the mean accuracy of T2/T1 across lags 2–5 (lag2 to lag5), calculated as AB = (lag2 + lag3 + lag4 + lag5)/4. Statistical analyses—including one-way repeated-measures ANOVA, post hoc tests (with T1 accuracy as baseline), 2×10 mixed-design ANOVA (group × position), partial correlation (controlling for age and flight time), and hierarchical regression (with AB as predictor and lowest flight performance score as outcome)—were conducted using SPSS 22.0."}
{'sample_description': "The study recruited participants using an opportunity sampling approach, comprising five groups: (1) an elite cricket group (female; n=16, age 25.0 ± 2.9 years), consisting of members of England’s national women’s cricket team; (2) a near-elite cricket group (male; n=28, age 21.0 ± 1.5 years), comprising members of the Leeds/Bradford Marylebone Cricket Club University (MCCU) squad—the top young university players from Yorkshire who compete against English county-level sides; (3) an elite rugby group (male; n=21, age 23.0 ± 4.0 years), consisting of professional 'Super-League' Rugby-League players; (4) a male control group (n=30, age 23.0 ± 7.0 years); and (5) a female control group (n=20, age 22.0 ± 4.0 years). Both control groups were University of Bradford students with no history of competitive ball-sport participation and no routine engagement in ball sports. All participants provided written informed consent, had normal or corrected-to-normal vision, and reported no known neurological or sensorimotor deficits.

All MATLAB-based analyses used custom scripts.'}
{'sample_description': 'A sample of S. schimperi seeds was collected from Menz Mama Midir district, North Shewa Zone, Ethiopia between January 15 and February 14, 2022; identified and recorded as voucher number MA001 at the National Herbarium, Addis Ababa University, College of Natural and Computational Sciences; the voucher specimen was deposited at the same herbarium; samples were manually cleaned, dried, packed in hermetic plastic vessels, and stored at 5°C until analysis; roasting was performed at 185°C for 15 minutes using an electrical drying oven to simulate traditional community usage, yielding both raw (untreated) and roasted (treated) seed samples for comparative metabolomic analysis.', 'SIRIUS_configuration_description': "SIRIUS software (v5.0, Windows version) was used for MS2-based compound annotation after importing the MGF file generated from MZmine preprocessing; molecular formula prediction was configured to search the entire database with ZODIAC, CSI:FingerID, and CANOPUS enabled; all other parameters were left at default values; the 'compute all' tab was selected to execute molecular formula prediction and structure elucidation; in the Sirius overview tab, the compound with the highest Sirius score was selected as the potential candidate; the 'export summarize' option was enabled to export MS2 annotation search results of recommended compounds; compound identification was based on confidence scores, with selection criteria requiring COSMIC confidence score > 0.1, ZODIAC score ≥ 50%, and Sirius score ≥ 50% (if no ZODIAC score was available); candidate structures were searched across Natural Products, COCONUT, and CHEBI databases via top CSI:FingerID hits; fingerprint vectors of top candidates were exported for further analysis; complementary use of COSMIC (for molecular structure annotation confidence) and CANOPUS (for broad chemical class classification) was applied to strengthen annotation reliability.", 'process_procedure_description': 'The untargeted metabolomics workflow involved multiple software tools: (1) MZmine (v3.7.0, Windows) was used for raw LC-MS data preprocessing, including mass detection (centroid), chromatogram building (ADAP with scan-to-scan accuracy set to 0.005 Da or 10 ppm), deisotoping, peak alignment, and missing peak filling; noise level parameters were set to 2.0E3 for MS1 and 3.0E2 for MS2; resulting MS1 features were exported as Excel (.csv) files and MS2 features as MGF files in SIRIUS/CSI:FingerID format; (2) XCMS Online (https://xcmsonline.scripps.edu/) was used with pairwise drop-down job creation and HPLC/single quad parameter selection for feature extraction, alignment, and annotation via METLIN; (3) XCMS R package (v3.20.0) was executed via command line for equivalent feature extraction, alignment, and data analysis; (4) MetaboAnalyst 5.0 was used for statistical analysis of XCMS Online output CSV files, applying sum normalization and auto-scaling, followed by PCA (5 components) and OPLS-DA; signature metabolites were defined by p-value < 0.05, FDR-corrected q-value < 0.05, fold change > 2.0, and VIP > 1.2; tenfold cross-validation assessed model accuracy (Q² and R²), and permutation testing validated model significance (permutation p-value < 0.01).'}
{'sample_description': 'The study included 68 filamentous fungal isolates collected from clinical samples and quality control isolates (UK NEQAS) sent to the Mycology Section, Department of Clinical Microbiology at Karolinska University Hospital between 2020 and 2024. These comprised 47 clinical isolates and 21 quality control isolates, representing 43 species across 12 genera including Aspergillus spp. (24 isolates), Order Mucorales (8), Talaromyces spp. (5), Penicillium spp. (3), Scedosporium spp. (7), Fusarium spp. (7), Acremonium spp. (2), Lomentospora prolificans (2), Scopulariopsis brevicaulis (2), Cladosporium pseudocladosporoides (1), Chaetomium bostrychodes (1), Rasamsonia spp. (1), Trichoderma citrinoviridae (1), and Purpureocillium lilacinum (2).

', 'SIRIUS_configuration_description': 'Compound identification of metabolites from the ethanol extract was performed using the Sirius platform (Zheng et al., 2023; Pang et al., 2024). Raw LCMS data were first converted and processed using MZmine platform for peak detection, deconvolution, alignment, and filtering. Subsequently, the processed peak list (including retention time, m/z, intensity, and MS/MS spectra) was imported into Sirius for molecular formula annotation, structure elucidation, and similarity scoring. Sirius was configured to perform CSI:FingerID-based structural database searching and ZODIAC-based molecular formula ranking, with compound annotations validated against spectral matching scores (Sirius similarity % reported per compound in Table 2, ranging from 59% to 100%). The analysis included fragmentation tree computation and molecular fingerprint prediction to support structural assignments of nine predicted compounds, including betulinic acid, ursolic acid, ceratodictyol, and nervisterol.', 'process_procedure_description': 'The metabolite profiling workflow involved multiple software-integrated steps: (1) Data acquisition was performed on a Thermo Scientific Orbitrap Fusion mass spectrometer coupled with UHPLC; (2) Raw LCMS data (.raw files) were converted to open formats (e.g., mzML) and processed using MZmine 3.4.0 (or later compatible version) for chromatogram building, peak detection (with noise level set to 5e3, minimum peak width 0.05–0.5 min, ADAP chromatogram builder), deconvolution (ADAP), alignment (join aligner with 0.2 min RT tolerance and 5 ppm m/z tolerance), and gap filling; (3) Processed feature tables (CSV format containing m/z, RT, intensity, and MS/MS spectra) were exported and imported into Sirius 4.8.1 (or later) for molecular formula prediction (using ZODIAC), structure identification (via CSI:FingerID against PubChem and GNPS databases), and confidence scoring (Sirius similarity %); (4) All statistical analysis of cytotoxicity data—including IC50 calculation, dose-response curve fitting, and significance testing—was performed using GraphPad Prism Software v9.0.0 (GraphPad Software LLC), applying nonlinear regression (log(inhibitor) vs. response – Variable slope model) with triplicate biological replicates and error expressed as mean ± SD.'}
