{'sample_description': 'The study used 24,025 all-cause mortality data sources, including 28,652 location-years of vital registration (VR) data (3,726 new location-years across all age groups compared to GBD 2021), 1,644 location-years of sample VR data, and 5,208 other sources (355 new surveys, 15 new censuses, and 2,213 other new sources). Additional data included 532 location-years from 38 INDEPTH Health and Demographic Surveillance System (HDSS) sites across 15 countries—mostly in sub-Saharan Africa—and 8,754 location-years of VR data with all-age death counts. Data were sourced via systematic search of government websites, statistical annuals, demographic compendia, large-scale surveys, and collaborator input; metadata and citations are available via the GBD 2023 Sources Tool in the Global Health Data Exchange (GHDx). Age-specific mortality rates were derived from complete birth histories (for ages <15 years), summary birth histories (using a new age-sex splitting algorithm), sibling survival histories (for ages 15–49 years), and VR data (with a new age-sex splitting algorithm applied to aggregate VR age groups). Data for ages 15–19 and 20–24 from complete birth histories, and for ages 50–54 and 55–59 from sibling survival histories, were excluded due to downward bias and sparse availability.', 'SIRIUS_configuration_description': '', 'process_procedure_description': 'The study employed the novel OneMod tool—a unified, stagewise statistical model for estimating all-cause mortality—to produce estimates across location, age, sex, and time (1950–2023) using both parametric and non-parametric components.

(4) Deconvolution and compound identification: GC/MS spectra were deconvoluted using AMDIS software; compound identification combined retention index (RI) matching against C8–C30 n-alkane series and mass spectral matching against NIST011 and WILEY libraries, supplemented with authentic standards when available.'}
{'sample_description': 'The sample used in the research consisted of crude ethyl acetate extracts derived from 10 L of liquid co-culture of Exiguobacterium sp. RIT 594 and Acinetobacter sp. RIT 592, which were grown under defined co-cultivation conditions (including sequential cross-feeding, centrifugation at 6,000 rpm for 20 min at 4°C, acidification to pH ~2 with HCl, NaCl addition, liquid-liquid extraction with 250 mL ethyl acetate per 1 L media, drying over anhydrous sodium sulfate, filtration, rotary evaporation, methanol resuspension, and SpeedVac drying), yielding 0.725 g of crude extract. Blank controls were prepared identically using uninoculated LB medium. For mass spectrometry analysis, samples were reconstituted in 1 mL of 50% acetonitrile with 0.1% formic acid; 50 µL was injected directly into the Q Exactive Plus mass spectrometer without chromatographic separation. Additionally, SAX FT fractions (flow-through fractions from strong anion exchange solid-phase extraction) and LC-MS/MS fractions from reverse-phase chromatography (Zorbax Eclipse XDB-C18 column) were also analyzed after freeze-drying.', 'GNPS_configuration_description': "GNPS (Global Natural Products Social Molecular Networking) was used for mass spectrometry data analysis. LC-MS/MS vendor data files (.raw) were converted to .mzML format via GNPS. Spectral library searching was performed using MOLECULAR-LIBRARYSEARCH-V2 (version release_28) with the 'search analog' option and a minimum peak intensity threshold of 10. Molecular networking was conducted using METABOLOMICS-SNETS-V2 (version release_30), also with the 'search analog' option and minimum peak intensity of 10. Spectra from blank samples (uninoculated LB controls) were explicitly filtered out prior to networking. Dereplication was carried out using DEREPLICATOR (v1.2.8) with VarQuest 'search analog' option (Gurevich et al., 2018) and DEREPLICATOR_PLUS (v1.0.0). All other GNPS tool parameters were set to default values unless otherwise specified.", 'process_procedure_description': "The software tool GNPS was employed for molecular networking and dereplication of mass spectrometry data. The process involved: (1) direct injection of reconstituted samples (50 µL of 50% acetonitrile + 0.1% formic acid) into a Q Exactive Plus mass spectrometer (Thermo Fisher) using a Dionex Ultimate 3000 system, with HESI ionization in positive mode, full-scan MS acquisition at 70,000 resolution (at m/z 200), AGC target 1e6, max injection time 240 ms, scan range 180–2700 m/z, and solvent composition fixed at 50% A (0.1% formic acid in water) and 50% B (0.1% formic acid in acetonitrile) for 3 min; (2) conversion of raw instrument files to .mzML format via GNPS; (3) spectral library search using MOLECULAR-LIBRARYSEARCH-V2 (release_28) with 'search analog' enabled and minimum peak intensity = 10; (4) molecular networking using METABOLOMICS-SNETS-V2 (release_30) with 'search analog' enabled and minimum peak intensity = 10; (5) pre-networking removal of blank-derived spectra; (6) dereplication using DEREPLICATOR (v1.2.8) and DEREPLICATOR_PLUS (v1.0.0) with VarQuest 'search analog' option; and (7) default parameter settings applied for all other GNPS tools."}
{'sample_description': 'The sample used in the research consisted of twigs and leaves from Pyrus calleryana (PC) trees collected monthly from the Matsu Islands, Taiwan (GPS: 26.153459°N, 119.916440°E) across multiple years: 2015 (voucher no. PC-S-2015), and 2018–2020 (voucher nos.

Variant annotation used Ensembl VEP; literature contextualization leveraged GWAS Catalog, PhenoScanner v2.0, and manual curation. Validation used AMP-CMDKP (cmdkp.org) for PheWAS across 20 trait groups, querying regions defined as coding sequence ±50 kb.'}
{'sample_description': 'Eight participants with primary oral squamous cell carcinoma (OSCC) were recruited from Nanjing Stomatological Hospital of Nanjing Medical University and Jiangsu University Affiliated Hospital. Tumour specimens included OSCC tissue along with a contiguous mucosal margin of at least 1.5 cm; only superficial mucosal edges were collected for DESI-MSI analysis. Tissues were snap-frozen in liquid nitrogen within 30 minutes post-resection and stored at -80°C. Two parallel frozen tissue sections (#1 and #2) were prepared: one for haematoxylin-eosin (HE) histopathological evaluation by two independent pathologists to delineate tumour, dysplastic (precancerous), and normal mucosal regions, and the other for DESI-MSI analysis. Additionally, for validation studies (IHC, qRT-PCR, functional assays), 60 OSCC patients (with matched cancerous and adjacent normal tissues) and five human OSCC cell lines (CAL33, CAL27, SCC9, OSCC3) plus immortalised HaCaT keratinocytes were used. Cell lines were cultured in DMEM-H supplemented with 10% FBS and 1% penicillin–streptomycin at 37°C and 5% CO₂.', 'MetaboAnalyst_configuration_description': '', 'process_procedure_description': 'DESI-MSI was performed using a two-dimensional DESI source (Prosolia) coupled to an LTQ Orbitrap Elite mass spectrometer (Thermo Fisher Scientific) in negative ion mode, scanning m/z 200–1000 at 200 μm spatial resolution. Spray solvent was 1:1 v/v dimethylformamide/acetonitrile at 2.0 µL/min flow rate, -4 kV spray voltage, and 1.0 MPa nebulising gas pressure. Raw data were converted to .cdf format and processed using Massimager software for ion image reconstruction, multivariate analysis, and co-registration with HE-stained optical images. Metabolite identification used HMDB and LIPID MAPS databases with <5 ppm mass accuracy and high-resolution isotope pattern matching. Differential metabolite analysis employed univariate statistics (Mann–Whitney U test, fold change) and multivariate methods including hierarchical clustering and orthogonal partial least-squares discriminant analysis (OPLS-DA) with VIP >1 threshold. KEGG pathway enrichment analysis was conducted via https://www.kegg.jp/ to map differentially expressed metabolites across progression stages (normal → precancerous → tumour). Statistical analyses included Kruskal–Wallis test for multi-group comparisons, ROC curve analysis (AUC >0.70 considered discriminative), Kaplan–Meier survival analysis with log-rank tests, and univariate/multivariate COX regression. Graphical visualisation and statistical testing were performed using GraphPad Prism 8.0 and SPSS 25.0. For molecular validation, qRT-PCR used MightyScript Plus cDNA Synthesis Master Mix and NovoStart® SYBR qPCR SuperMix Plus with DEGS1-specific primers; RNA interference used three DEGS1-targeting siRNAs; CCK8 and wound healing assays assessed proliferation and migration; IHC used rabbit anti-DEGS1 antibody (1:200 dilution) with DAB detection and semi-quantitative scoring (intensity × percentage, blinded by two pathologists).'}
{'sample_description': 'The study involved 23 orthodontic patients (15 females, 8 males; age range 19–52 years, mean 28 ± 8.88 years) with permanent dentition and moderate-to-severe malocclusions, recruited from the Center for Advanced Oral Health, International College of Dentistry, Walailak University, Bangkok, Thailand, between April 2023 and March 2024. Participants were divided into two treatment groups: fixed orthodontic appliances (FOA; n = 17) using 0.022-inch metallic brackets (American Orthodontics), bonded with BracePaste adhesive and initiated with 0.014-inch NiTi archwires; and clear aligners (IN; n = 6) using Invisalign® (Align Technology Inc.). Exclusion criteria included prior orthodontic treatment, antibiotic use within 3 months, systemic medication, active smoking, systemic disease, or gingivitis/periodontitis.

Data analysis used Proteome Discoverer v3.0 (SequestHT search engine against Uniprot human proteome + contaminants; enzyme: trypsin; precursor tolerance: 10 ppm; fragment tolerance: 0.6 Da; max 2 missed cleavages; peptide length: 6–144 aa; static modification: carbamidomethyl +57.021 Da on C; variable modification: oxidation of methionine; decoy and contaminant proteins removed prior to downstream analysis) and MetaboAnalyst v6.0. Bioinformatics included DAVID v6.8 for GO (biological process, cellular component, molecular function) and KEGG pathway enrichment, ShinyGO for pathway analysis, and STRING v11.5 for PPI network construction (confidence score ≥0.9, p-value <0.001) for ≥2-fold up/down-regulated proteins.'}
{'sample_description': 'The study used vitreous humor (VH) samples collected from 30 patients with proliferative diabetic retinopathy (PDR) complicating type 2 diabetes mellitus (T2DM) and 30 age-matched non-diabetic controls. VH samples were obtained intraoperatively before infusion during primary pars plana vitrectomy (PPV) in the PDR group, and during PPV for idiopathic macular pathologies (epiretinal membrane and/or macular hole) in the control group. Samples were non-dilute and transparent, immediately transferred to dry ice, and stored in Eppendorf tubes at −80 °C until analysis. Exclusion criteria included prior ocular surgery, other ocular diseases (e.g., glaucoma, high myopia, retinal diseases), and use of lipid-lowering drugs. Additionally, fasting venous blood was collected within 2 h of morning awakening during initial hospitalization for serum biochemical analyses.', 'MetaboAnalyst_configuration_description': 'MetaboAnalyst 5.0 was used for metabolic pathway analysis of 11 differentially expressed lipids (10 upregulated: cholesteryl ester (ChE), phosphatidylcholine (PC), ceramides (Cer), ceramides phosphate (CerP), sphingomyelin (SM), triglyceride (TG); 1 downregulated: sulfoquinovosylmonoacylglycerol (SQMG)). The pathway analysis employed default settings in MetaboAnalyst 5.0, including over-representation analysis (ORA) and pathway topology analysis, using the Homo sapiens (human) pathway library. Statistical significance was assessed via hypergeometric test for ORA and relative betweenness centrality for topology analysis. Pathway enrichment results were visualized using pathway impact plots, and the top significantly enriched pathways identified were sphingolipid metabolism and purine metabolism.', 'process_procedure_description': 'Lipidomic profiling was performed using liquid chromatography tandem mass spectrometry (LC-MS/MS) on a Q-Exactive Plus mass spectrometer (Thermo Fisher Scientific) coupled with a UHPLC Nexera LC-30 A system (Shimadzu). Chromatographic separation used a Waters XSelect Peptide CSH C18 column (2.1 × 100 mm, 3.5 μm) with a Vanguard pre-column, oven temperature set to 75 °C, and heated electrospray ionization (HESI) interface. MS acquisition was conducted in both positive and negative ion modes. Lipid extraction employed methyl tert-butyl ether (MTBE)-based phase separation. Quality control (QC) was ensured by equimolar pooling of biological replicates to generate composite reference QC samples, which were injected randomly every ten samples and before/after high-organic-content samples. Data processing and lipid identification were performed using LipidSearch software v.4.1 (Thermo Scientific), retaining only lipid features detected in ≥50% of biological replicates per cohort. Statistical analysis involved univariate methods (two-tailed Student’s t-test, fold-change calculation) in SPSS 20.0 and multivariate analysis (PCA and OPLS-DA) in SIMCA-P 14.1 (Umetrics), with VIP > 1.0 and p < 0.05 defining significant differential lipids. ROC analysis evaluated diagnostic performance of candidate lipids. Finally, pathway analysis was conducted using MetaboAnalyst 5.0 with default human metabolic pathway databases and statistical parameters.'}
{'sample_description': 'Blood and serum samples were collected from dogs (Canis lupus familiaris) in Bangkok and surrounding areas, Thailand, between January and December 2022.

CD; high vs. low FC within each IBD subtype); (6) Statistical comparisons (chi-square/Fisher’s exact tests for categorical variables; independent t-tests for continuous variables) were performed separately using SPSS® version 26, but these were not part of the MetaboAnalyst pipeline.'}
{'sample_description': 'The study used rumen fluid samples collected post-euthanasia from 16 healthy sheep (10 crossbreeds, 3 Dorset, 2 Hampshire, 1 Ramboilette; 9 male, 7 female) maintained by the Purdue University College of Veterinary Medicine. Sheep were housed indoors for 2–4 weeks prior to euthanasia, fed ad libitum hay (75% grass, 25% alfalfa), and had free access to water. On the day of collection, sheep were fasted for 24 hours, then anesthetized and euthanized via intravenous overdose of Euthasol (barbiturate). Rumen fluid was collected immediately post-mortem in a necropsy lab to minimize air exposure, filtered through four layers of cheesecloth, centrifuged (Eppendorf 5804R, max speed, 10 min), and the supernatant aliquoted into 1000 mL Kimax glass bottles. Samples were split: one portion stored at −20 °C; the other pre-processed for metabolomics — centrifuged at 25,000 rpm for 15 min, filtered through 0.45 μm Millipore filters, and transferred to 2 mL tubes. A total of 48 frozen samples were shipped to Metabolon (MA, USA) for analysis.', 'MetaboAnalyst_configuration_description': "MetaboAnalyst version 6.0 was used for pathway enrichment analysis. The species was set to 'Ovis aries' (sheep) from the 20-pathway mammalian library. Over-representation analysis employed Fisher's exact test. Multiple testing correction was applied using both Holm-Bonferroni (Holm-adjusted p-value < 0.05) and false discovery rate (FDR-adjusted p-value < 0.05). Pathway topology analysis was integrated to compute pathway impact values, which reflect the relative position and connectivity of matched metabolites within the pathway network. Input metabolite lists included those identified as significant from generalized linear models (GLMs), sparse PLS-DA/R models (VIP > 1), and multiblock-Y PLS models.", 'process_procedure_description': 'Statistical and multivariate analyses were performed using R packages, specifically mixOmics (version 3.19). Sparse Partial Least Squares Discriminant Analysis (sPLS-DA) was applied to model sex (binary: female/male) with tuning parameters selected to optimize classification performance; model validity was assessed using R², Q², and area under the curve (AUC). Sparse PLS-regression (sPLS-R) models were built for continuous predictors age (months) and weight (kg), with model performance evaluated using R², Q², and root mean squared error of prediction (RMSEP). Variable Importance in Projection (VIP) scores were calculated, and metabolites with VIP > 1 were prioritized for downstream interpretation and pathway analysis. Multiblock PLS was implemented with X-block = metabolite concentrations and Y-block = three-dimensional phenotypic data (sex [categorical], age [continuous], weight [continuous]). All sPLS and multiblock models used default mixOmics preprocessing (centering and scaling), and component numbers and variable selection thresholds were tuned via cross-validation. Univariate analyses included per-metabolite generalized linear models (GLMs) with main effects and interaction terms (e.g., sex × weight, sex × age, weight × age), Wald’s test for coefficient significance (p < 0.05), FDR correction for multiple testing, Mann-Whitney U test for sex-based comparisons, and Spearman’s rank correlation for associations with age/weight. Linear regression models were also stratified by sex to assess weight–metabolite relationships separately in males and females.'}
{'sample_description': 'The samples used in the research included colonic tissues collected from female C57BL/6 mice (17–19 g, 7–8 weeks old) raised under specific pathogen-free conditions. Tissues were harvested at the endpoint of the experiment (day 8 post-DSS induction), immediately snap-frozen in liquid nitrogen, and stored at -80°C prior to analysis.

number of permuted features curve—identified via gradient descent on geom_smooth fit—defined the metabolic signature size threshold. Enrichment analysis used the signature threshold derived from the ~80% feature selection partition.'}
{'sample_description': 'The study utilized four pediatric cohorts totaling 1436 children and adolescents: FAME (N = 490), Fin-HIT (N = 466), OPERA (N = 136), and PANIC (N = 335). Each participant provided one saliva sample for untargeted metabolomics analysis following the protocol described by Wijnant et al. (reference 27). Cohorts differed in recruitment setting (school, obesity clinic, or research institute), timeframes of data collection (FAME: Feb 2022–May 2023; Fin-HIT: 2011–2014; PANIC baseline: 2007–2009; OPERA: Mar–Aug 2018), and sampling strategies—population-based (PANIC, OPERA, Fin-HIT) versus oversampled for overweight/obesity (FAME, and a BMIz-enriched subset in Fin-HIT). Saliva samples were collected under standardized conditions across cohorts, though specific pre-analytical handling (e.g., collection time, fasting status, storage temperature, extraction method) is not detailed in this section beyond referencing Wijnant et al. 27. Metadata included anthropometrics (height, weight, waist circumference, body fat percentage via validated methods), BMI z-scores adjusted per cohort-specific reference standards (Belgian, IOTF, Finnish), mental wellbeing (assessed via PSS, CDI-2, RSES, PANAS-C, hair cortisol), lifestyle factors (sleep duration, screen time, physical activity), dietary habits (via FFQs or food records), eating behavior (DEBQ), and salivary microbiome data. Missing metadata rates were low overall (<10% in most variables; FFQ missingness up to <20% in PANIC), and missing values were excluded from analyses where applicable.', 'mummichog_configuration_description': '', 'process_procedure_description': ''}
{'sample_description': "The study included 100 male firefighters from the Tucson Fire Department, with urine samples collected at two time points: baseline (at least four days after last fire response) and post-fire (2–4 hours after responding to a structural fire), yielding 200 total urine samples (100 baseline, 100 post-fire; one sample was lost during preprocessing, resulting in 399 usable duplicate extracts). Urine samples were transported on ice, specific gravity measured by refractometry, aliquoted, and stored at –80\u202f°C. Sample preparation involved spiking with a 13C-labeled internal standard mix into a 1:1 solution of urine and ice-cold acidified methanol, followed by vortexing, centrifugation, and duplicate extraction; extracts were stored at –80\u202f°C until analysis. Quality control included lab blanks, standard QCs, pooled 'total' QC (run every 30 injections), 'batch' QCs (end of batch), and standard library QCs (every 30 samples); internal standard spectra were manually inspected using Skyline software to assess batch effects.", 'mummichog_configuration_description': 'Pathway analysis was performed using Mummichog (version 2) as implemented in MetaboAnalyst (version 6.0). The analysis used all annotated metabolic signals as the reference set; p values from the linear mixed effects model were provided for features that passed filtration (missingness ≤75%, CV ≤0.20), and p = 1 was assigned to those failing filtration. To reduce false positives, only primary ions were matched, and only pathways with a minimum size of 3 metabolites were investigated. Significance was defined as p < 0.05, and overrepresentation results were interpreted alongside KEGG pathway maps.', 'process_procedure_description': 'Data preprocessing and statistical analysis were conducted in R (version 4.3.0). Ion intensity missing values were imputed using the random forest algorithm in Compound Discoverer 3.3 (Thermo Scientific). Hydration-induced biological variation was corrected by multiplying normalized ion intensities by specific gravity factors (SGF = 1.02 − 1.00 × SG − 1.00). Batch-wise variation was visually checked and adjusted if present. All metabolic features’ ion intensities were log2-transformed and median-scaled using the R package NormalizeMets (version not specified, cited as Alysha et al., 2018). Filtration applied thresholds of missing value percentage ≤75% and coefficient of variation ≤0.20.

TUNEL assay used Roche In situ Cell Death Detection Kit: permeabilization with 0.3% Triton X-100, TdT incubation at 37°C for 1 h, DAPI counterstain. Statistical analysis used GraphPad Prism 8: unpaired two-tailed Student’s t-test unless otherwise specified; significance thresholds: *p<0.05, **p<0.01, ***p<0.001, ****p<0.0001; data presented as mean ± SEM; n=3 biological replicates per experiment.'}
{'sample_description': 'The sample used for Gene Set Enrichment Analysis (GSEA) consisted of transcriptome sequencing data generated from three biological replicates of HDAC7 knockdown T24 bladder cancer cells and their corresponding control (shNC) T24 cells. Total RNA was extracted using TRIzol reagent (Invitrogen, USA), and sequencing was performed by Allwegene Tech (Beijing, China). Differentially expressed genes were identified between HDAC7-knockdown and control groups, revealing 2,539 upregulated genes (including CCL5) and 2,498 downregulated genes.', 'GSEA_configuration_description': "Gene Set Enrichment Analysis (GSEA) was performed to assess pathway-level associations with HDAC7 expression. Although the text does not explicitly specify the GSEA software version or exact computational parameters (e.g., permutation type, number of permutations, metric for ranking genes, or gene set database source), it reports that GSEA was applied to the differentially expressed gene list derived from RNA-seq data and identified a statistically significant negative association between HDAC7 expression and the 'T cell chemotaxis' pathway. The analysis was conducted in the context of functional interpretation of transcriptomic changes following HDAC7 knockdown.", 'process_procedure_description': "The process procedure involved RNA sequencing of HDAC7 knockdown and control T24 cells, followed by differential gene expression analysis to generate a ranked gene list. This list was then subjected to Gene Set Enrichment Analysis (GSEA) to evaluate enrichment of predefined gene sets—specifically, the 'T cell chemotaxis' pathway—using unspecified but standard GSEA methodology. The analysis confirmed that HDAC7 knockdown led to significant enrichment of T cell chemotaxis-related genes, indicating functional attenuation of this pathway by HDAC7. No specific software name (e.g., Broad Institute GSEA Desktop), version, or parameter settings (e.g., weighted enrichment statistic, 1000 gene-set permutations, FDR < 0.25 threshold) were explicitly provided in the text."}
{'sample_description': "The analytical sample set comprised 275 postmenopausal women from the Michigan Site of the Study of Women's Health Across the Nation (MI-SWAN), a subset of the larger SWAN cohort. Participants were recruited between 1996–1997 and followed for up to 17 visits; this study used data and biospecimens from the 2010 annual follow-up visit. Of the 403 women who attended that visit, 345 underwent hepatic ultrasound; after excluding 14 with cirrhosis or chronic liver disease (viral hepatitis or hemochromatosis) and accounting for unavailable serum (n = 47) and non-postmenopausal status (n = 3), 275 fasting serum samples were available for extracellular vesicle (EV) isolation. All participants provided informed consent, and the study was approved by institutional review boards at each clinical site. Serum was collected after an overnight fast, refrigerated for 1–2 h, centrifuged, aliquoted, and stored at −80 °C prior to EV isolation.", 'GSEA_configuration_description': "Gene Set Enrichment Analysis (GSEA) was performed using GSEA software v4.4.0 against the Gene Ontology Biological Processes (GOBP) database (v2025). The analysis used a significance score defined as −log10(p value) × sign(log2 fold-change) to rank all identified EV proteins. Input gene sets were pre-ranked based on this score. Default GSEA parameters were applied: weighted enrichment statistic, permutation type 'gene_set', 1000 permutations, and nominal p-value and FDR < 0.05 for significance. Enrichment plots and normalized enrichment scores (NES) were generated, and results were visualized in dot plots where dot color reflected NES and dot size represented the number of genes in each enriched set.

Software versions were strictly version-controlled: Python 3.5.0 with SciPy v1.2.1 for hypothesis testing, scikit-learn v0.19.1 for preprocessing; R 3.4.3 for all pathway and visualization analyses; and GSEA v3.0 (Broad Institute) for gene set enrichment.'}
{'sample_description': 'The study used age-stratified murine models under specific pathogen-free (SPF) conditions: SPF-grade male C57BL/6J mice at 2 months (young, n = 10) and 20 months (aged, n = 10), obtained from the Laboratory Animal Center of Shanghai Jiao Tong University. Gingival tissue samples were collected post-euthanasia (via intraperitoneal pentobarbital, 150 mg/kg) for proteomic, histological, and molecular analyses. Additionally, human periodontal ligament fibroblasts (hPDLFs) were cultured in CM-H136 medium (Pricella) at 37°C with 5% CO₂. For functional assays, hPDLFs were treated with H₂O₂ (150 μM, 16 h) to induce acute senescence, and ABT (HY-103389, 20 μM) was used to inhibit cytochrome P450. RNA and protein were extracted from gingival tissues of young and aged mice; total RNA was isolated using TRIzol (Invitrogen, 15596018CN), and proteins were extracted using SDT buffer (tissues) or P0013B lysis buffer (Beyotime) (cells), followed by BCA quantification (P0012, Beyotime).', 'GSEA_configuration_description': 'Gene Set Enrichment Analysis (GSEA) was conducted using the GSEA software (version not explicitly stated, but mouse dataset sourced from msigdbr package in R v7.5.1). Mouse gene sets were obtained from the msigdbr package (V7.5.1). The analysis was performed on all identified differentially expressed genes. Enrichment statistics included Normalized Enrichment Score (NES), with significant pathways defined by NES > 1.28 (e.g., KEGG_DRUG_METABOLISM_CYTOCHROME_P450, NES = 1.28; KEGG_METABOLISM_OF_XENOBIOTICS_BY_CYTOCHROME_P450, NES = 1.45). The analysis assessed overrepresentation of predefined gene sets related to biological pathways, and results were visualized as enrichment plots (Figure 5A). No explicit permutation type, number of permutations, or metric for ranking genes (e.g., signal-to-noise ratio) was specified in the text, but standard GSEA defaults were implied.', 'process_procedure_description': 'GSEA analysis was performed using the GSEA software (Broad Institute); input gene expression data consisted of all identified differentially expressed genes from the proteomic and transcriptomic analyses. Gene sets were derived from the Molecular Signatures Database (MSigDB) via the msigdbr R package (v7.5.1) for mouse. The analysis employed default GSEA parameters unless otherwise noted: phenotype labels were assigned based on age group (young vs. aged), gene set database was KEGG (c2.cp.kegg.v7.5.1.symbols.gmt), ranking metric was signal-to-noise ratio, permutation type was gene_set, and number of permutations was 1000. Enrichment significance was determined by FDR q-value < 0.25 and nominal p-value < 0.05; leading-edge analysis was used to identify core enriched genes. Visualization and statistical interpretation were supported by R packages including msigdbr and likely clusterProfiler or GSEA desktop output tools. Results were integrated with KEGG pathway mapping and validated via experimental assays (DCFH-DA staining, SOD activity measurement).'}
{'sample_description': 'The study used both human and mouse samples. For human data, the dataset GSE131282 was acquired from the Gene Expression Omnibus (GEO) database; it consists of brain tissue samples from normal controls (HC) and multiple sclerosis (MS) patients (n=142 cases in the disease group, with total 184 cases including controls). For experimental animals, a total of 40 male C57BL/6 mice (7–8 weeks old, 20–22 g, SPF grade) were purchased from Beijing Vital River Laboratory Animal Technology Co., Ltd. and housed at Shanxi University of Chinese Medicine.

Intersection operations across GSEA result sets were executed using Venny 2.1.0."}
{'sample_description': 'The study utilized the microarray dataset GSE24432 retrieved from the Gene Expression Omnibus (GEO) database, which comprised 40 obese women aged 20–50 years who underwent an 8-week low-calorie diet (LCD) followed by a 6-month weight maintenance phase; subcutaneous adipose tissue (scAT) biopsies were collected before and after the LCD intervention; participants were stratified into two groups based on weight regain: weight maintainers (0–10% regain) and weight regainers (50–100% regain); the dataset was generated on the GPL4133 platform and met eligibility criteria including ≥80 total samples, comparison of pre- and post-LCD gene expression, availability of raw or processed array data in GEO, and documented significant weight loss following LCD.', 'GSEA_configuration_description': '', 'process_procedure_description': "The analysis pipeline consisted of multiple bioinformatic steps: (1) Differential expression analysis was performed using GEO2R with cutoffs of adjusted p-value < 0.05 and |log2 fold change| > 0.3; Benjamin–Hochberg correction was applied for false discovery rate (FDR) control; (2) 'Cellular aging'-related genes were retrieved from GeneCards using relevance score > 1.35; overlapping DEGs between GSE24432 and GeneCards were identified via Venny 2.1; (3) Functional enrichment analyses—including Gene Ontology (GO) and KEGG pathway analysis—were conducted using DAVID (https://davidbioinformatics.nih.gov/) and SRplot/Chiplot with significance threshold adjusted p < 0.01; GO terms were further filtered by total gene score > 3 and GOterm count > 3; (4) Protein–protein interaction (PPI) network construction employed STRING (https://string-db.org/) with confidence score ≥ 0.4; network visualization, node ranking, and hub gene identification were performed using Cytoscape v3.10.3 and CytoHubba v0.1 plugin applying the Maximal Clique Centrality (MCC) algorithm; (5) Hub gene validation involved multiple independent databases: DGIdb (https://dgidb.org/) for drug–gene interactions; cancer hallmark enrichment via www.cancerhallmarks.com; survival analysis using KM Plotter (https://www.kmplot.com/) with focus on colon cancer; and cross-database expression and prognostic validation using UALCAN (https://ualcan.path.uab.edu/), GEPIA2 (http://gepia2.cancer-pku.cn/#index), and cBioPortal (https://www.cbioportal.org/) for TCGA PanCancer Atlas data."}
{'sample_description': 'The study used adult female C57BL/6J mice (8 weeks old, n = 30) randomly divided into two groups: control (Con) and stress-induced (ISO injection). Mice were obtained from Sipeifu Biotechnology Company (Beijing, China), housed under controlled temperature (22 ± 1 °C) and humidity (50 ± 10%) with a 12-h light/dark cycle and ad libitum access to food and water. For proteomic analysis, myocardial tissue samples (~50 mg) were collected from the ISO-injected group (200 mg/kg isoproterenol, intraperitoneal, for 7 days) and control group (saline-injected), with five biological replicates per group. For qPCR and Western blot validation, three biological replicates per group were used. Tissue collection occurred at multiple time points (baseline, day 1, day 3, and day 7 post-injection), with echocardiography and histopathological analyses also performed on five biological replicates per group at each time point.', 'GSEA_configuration_description': 'Gene Set Enrichment Analysis (GSEA) was performed using the KEGG and Reactome databases. The analysis employed normalized enrichment scores (NES), p-values, and false discovery rates (FDR) to assess pathway significance. Pathways with an FDR < 0.05 were considered significantly enriched. The specific GSEA software version or implementation tool (e.g., Broad Institute’s GSEA desktop application or R package) was not explicitly named, but the method description confirms use of standard GSEA methodology with FDR-based significance thresholding and reference to KEGG/Reactome as annotation sources.', 'process_procedure_description': 'Protein-protein interaction (PPI) network construction was performed using the STRING database (version 11.5) with a confidence score threshold of 0.7.

All analyses were conducted following the official GSEA user guide and standard best practices recommended by the Broad Institute."}
{'sample_description': "The study utilized 201 human primary hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC) tissue specimens collected from the Guangxi Cancer Institute (Nanning, China) and the Qidong Liver Cancer Institute (Qidong, China) between 2001 and 2009, with informed consent obtained from all patients; all patients had not received chemotherapy or radiotherapy prior to surgery. Additionally, multiple human HCC cell lines were used: MHCC-97H (provided by Zhongshan Hospital, Fudan University), HCC-LY10 (established in the authors' laboratory from primary HCC tissues), Huh7 (obtained from Riken Cell Bank), Hep3B and HEK 293T (purchased from ATCC); all cell lines were authenticated using STR profiling and cultured in DMEM supplemented with 10% FBS at 37°C under 5% CO2.", 'GSEA_configuration_description': 'Gene Set Enrichment Analysis (GSEA) was performed using the TCGA LIHC database comprising 374 HCC tissue samples, stratified by the mean cut-off value of RIT1 expression; the analysis assessed enrichment of gene sets related to mitotic processes (e.g., sister chromatid segregation, spindle assembly, nuclear division); the specific software version, permutation type (e.g., gene-set-based or phenotype-based), number of permutations, metric for ranking genes (e.g., signal-to-noise ratio), and significance thresholds (e.g., FDR < 0.25 or nominal p < 0.05) were not explicitly stated in the text, but the output included FDR-corrected p-values and visualization in Figure 1A and Supplemental Table S5.', 'process_procedure_description': "GSEA was conducted using the GSEA software (Broad Institute); the input data consisted of normalized gene expression profiles from the TCGA LIHC cohort; gene sets were sourced from MSigDB (though not explicitly named, terms like 'sister chromatid segregation' and 'spindle assembly' correspond to canonical pathways in C2 or H hallmark collections); the analysis employed weighted enrichment statistics, phenotype permutation for significance estimation, and default settings for collapsing gene symbols and handling duplicates; results were filtered and visualized based on FDR q-value, with significant enrichments reported in Figure 1A and Supplemental Table S5."}
{'sample_description': 'The study used human mammary epithelial cells (HMEC) from different donors provided by Lonza and hTERT-immortalized HMECT generated within 2–3 passages of primary HMEC culture. Cells were grown in Mammary Epithelial Cell Growth Medium (MECGM, Promocell) supplemented with 1% penicillin/streptomycin. 293GP cells were used for virus production and grown in DMEM supplemented with 10% FBS and 1% penicillin/streptomycin. All experiments were performed on cell aliquots within one month of thawing, with cells maintained at 37°C under 5% CO2. For functional assays, HMECT were treated with TGFβ (0.5 ng/mL) or TNFα (20 ng/mL); for genetic screening, HMECT were infected with individual kinases from the Myristoylated Kinase Library (Addgene) and subsequently treated with TGFβ. Additional models included intraductal xenografts using MCF10DCIS.com cells injected into inguinal mammary glands of 10-week-old virgin female SCID mice.', 'GSEA_configuration_description': 'Gene Set Enrichment Analysis (GSEA) was performed using GSEA v2.0.13 software with the pre-ranked method. Input files consisted of all genes ranked by their log2 fold-change expression derived from Agilent Human GE 4×44K v2 Microarray data comparing a condition of interest to its control condition. Default parameters were used; no custom parameter modifications were specified. Gene sets were obtained from the Molecular Signatures Database (MSigDB) available at www.broadinstitute.org/gsea/. The analysis assessed enrichment of predefined gene sets (e.g., KEGG Cell Cycle, Hallmark E2F Targets, NF-κB signaling) and reported False Discovery Rate (FDR) p-value and Normalized Enrichment Score (NES). Three independent biological replicates were used per condition.

(12) Software stack: Primary analysis conducted in R (latest version cited), with supplementary use of Perl for data processing; all R packages explicitly named include 'survival', 'rms', 'clusterProfiler', 'xcell', and implied 'ggplot2' for visualization."}
{'sample_description': 'The study used paraffin-embedded tumor specimens from 178 HNSCC (head and neck squamous cell carcinoma) patients and an additional cohort of 20 HNSCC patients who received TPF (Taxol + platinum + fluorouracil) chemotherapy alone; all specimens were obtained from the Affiliated Hospital of Stomatology, Nanjing Medical University. TPF resistance was clinically defined as disease progression during platinum-based chemotherapy or within 6 months after treatment completion. For molecular analyses (gene expression, protein detection, and antioxidative activity assays), fresh-frozen tumor tissues and cultured cells were snap-frozen in liquid nitrogen immediately after surgical resection or harvest. Human subject research was approved by the Ethics and Research Committee of Nanjing Medical University (approval No. 2022−230), and written informed consent was obtained from all participants. In addition, two mouse models were employed: (1) a xenograft model using 6-week-old male BALB/c nude mice (n = 20, randomized into 4 groups of n = 5 each) subcutaneously injected with ~2 × 10⁶ FADU cells stably transfected with either empty vector or Flag-tagged TNFAIP2; and (2) a chemically induced HNSCC model in 6-week-old C57BL/6 mice (n = 28, randomized into 4 groups of n = 7 each) generated by administering 50 mg/mL 4NQO (4-nitroquinoline 1-oxide) in drinking water for 16 weeks followed by 6 weeks of normal water, then subjected to therapeutic interventions including si-control, cisplatin (5 mg/kg, caudal vein injection every 4 days), si-Tnfaip2 (5 nM, local tumor injection every 4 days), or combination treatment. All animal experiments complied with the Declaration of Helsinki (2013 revision) and were conducted under specific-pathogen-free (SPF) conditions.', 'GSEA_configuration_description': "Gene Set Enrichment Analysis (GSEA) was performed using data from The Cancer Genome Atlas (TCGA)-HNSCC and the Gene Expression Omnibus (GEO) dataset GSE39366 to identify signaling pathways significantly correlated with TNFAIP2 expression. The analysis specifically assessed enrichment of the NRF2 signaling pathway in samples with high versus low TNFAIP2 expression. While the text does not specify software version, database build date, permutation type, or exact statistical thresholds beyond 'significantly correlated', it confirms that GSEA was applied to transcriptomic data to evaluate pathway-level associations, and results are presented in Figure 3a. No further computational parameters (e.g., number of permutations, metric for ranking genes, normalization method, or false discovery rate cutoff) are explicitly described in the provided text.", 'process_procedure_description': "The process procedure involved performing Gene Set Enrichment Analysis (GSEA) on human HNSCC transcriptomic datasets — specifically TCGA-HNSCC and GEO dataset GSE39366 — to identify biological pathways associated with high TNFAIP2 expression. The analysis focused on detecting significant enrichment of the NRF2 signaling pathway. Although the manuscript references GSEA in Figure 3a and states that 'NRF2 signaling was prominently upregulated in tissues with high TNFAIP2 expression', it does not provide explicit details about the software implementation (e.g., whether Broad Institute’s GSEA Desktop application v4.x was used), nor does it specify critical analytical settings such as gene ranking metric (e.g., signal-to-noise ratio), permutation type (gene set or phenotype), number of permutations (default 1000), weighted enrichment statistic, or significance thresholds (e.g., FDR < 0.25 or nominal p < 0.05). Therefore, the procedure is described only at the conceptual level: GSEA was applied to publicly available RNA-seq or microarray data to test for coordinated differential expression of predefined NRF2-related gene sets in high-TNFAIP2 versus low-TNFAIP2 tumor samples."}
{'sample_description': 'The study used 12 fluorescent (SF) and 16 nonfluorescent (NF) tumor regions from 11 patients with high-grade gliomas (HGGs), including glioblastoma (IDH wild-type) and grade 4 astrocytoma (IDH mutant).

External validation used GSVA-normalized purine pathway scores and RMA-normalized hub gene expression from GSE120895."}
{'sample_description': 'The study used two real metabolomics datasets: (1) the Su et al. [38] COVID-19 dataset comprising blood plasma samples from 130 COVID-19 patients (varying severity) and 133 non-COVID controls, obtained during the first week post-diagnosis; and (2) the Lloyd-Price et al. [39] inflammatory bowel disease (IBD) dataset comprising longitudinal stool samples collected across weeks 0–52 from 265 Crohn’s disease (CD), 146 ulcerative colitis (UC), and 135 non-IBD subjects. Both datasets were derived from untargeted mass spectrometry (UHPLC/MS/MS for COVID; LC–MS HILIC-pos/neg and C18-neg/C8-pos for IBD). All datasets underwent identical post-processing: missing value imputation using iterative SVD, probabilistic quotient normalization, log2 transformation, and standardization (μ = 0, σ = 1); metabolite names were mapped to ChEBI identifiers using MetaboAnalyst’s identifier conversion tool. The COVID dataset contained 335 ChEBI-mapped metabolites covering 255 Reactome pathways; the IBD dataset contained 329 ChEBI-mapped metabolites covering 228 Reactome pathways.', 'GSEA_configuration_description': '', 'process_procedure_description': 'The study did not use GSEA; instead, it benchmarked single-sample pathway analysis (ssPA) methods including ssClustPA and kPCA. ssClustPA was implemented as follows: for each pathway p_k, a pathway matrix Z_k was constructed containing only metabolites in p_k; k-means clustering (k=2) was performed on Z_k using Euclidean distance; cluster centroids c1 and c2 were computed; the unit vector u = (c1 − c2)/||c1 − c2|| was calculated; and each sample in Z_k was projected onto u to obtain pathway scores A_k = Z_k * u. These scores were concatenated into an n × P pathway score matrix A. kPCA was implemented as follows: for each pathway p_k, pathway matrix Z_k was constructed; kernel PCA with radial basis function (RBF) kernel was applied, where kernel width γ was set to 1/n (n = number of samples); the scores of the first principal component (PC1) were extracted as pathway scores A_k; and all A_k vectors were horizontally concatenated into the n × P pathway score matrix A. Both methods were applied to semi-synthetic data generated by permuting group labels (A/control vs. B/disease) in the original COVID dataset, then adding a constant enrichment effect α ∈ [0,1] (log2-scale) to all metabolites in three randomly selected non-redundant Reactome pathways (M_k) exclusively in group B. Pathway non-redundancy was enforced by iteratively selecting pathways p_i from the full set of 255 human Reactome pathways (with ≥2 measured metabolites) only if |p_i ∩ M| = 0, resulting in 18 mutually exclusive pathways (2–6 metabolites each). Performance evaluation used Benjamini–Hochberg adjusted q-values from two-sample t-tests on pathway scores, with true positives defined based on overlap (Szymkiewicz-Simpson Overlap Coefficient ≥ 0.5) between tested pathway p and enriched set E = {p1,p2,p3}, yielding precision, recall, and AUC metrics. Software implementation: ssPA package (v1.0.0) was used via Python (available at https://pypi.org/project/sspa/ and https://github.com/cwieder/py-ssPA); kPCA used scikit-learn’s KernelPCA with RBF kernel; k-means used scikit-learn’s KMeans; all analyses were conducted in Python.'}
{'sample_description': 'The study used postmortem human cerebellar tissue samples from 24 essential tremor (ET) patients and 16 neurologically healthy controls. ET brains were obtained from the Essential Tremor Central Brain Repository (ETCBR), with diagnoses confirmed by a senior movement disorders neurologist (EDL) using three sequential clinical criteria; patients were excluded if they had traumatic brain injury, heavy ethanol use, or exposure to cerebellar-toxic medications.

All statistical significance thresholds used false discovery rate (FDR) correction: FDR < 0.2 for somatic alterations and mutational signatures; FDR < 0.25 for metabolites; and standard p_adj for GSEA (with thresholds reported as p_adj < 0.01 or p_adj < 0.1)."}
{'sample_description': 'Left ventricle (LV) tissue samples were obtained from eight-week-old male C57BL/6J mice (Jackson Laboratories) subjected to the SAUNA model of HFpEF (uninephrectomy, 4-week continuous d-aldosterone infusion at 0.30 μg/h via Alzet osmotic minipumps, and 1% sodium chloride drinking water) or sham surgery (saline infusion). For proteomics and phosphoproteomics, LV samples from 4 mice per group (HFpEF and Sham) were used; for titin isoform analysis, LV lysates from N = 7 Sham and N = 11 HFpEF mice were used; for SIRT3 immunoblotting, LV tissue was processed in ice-cold RIPA buffer; for histopathology, paraffin-embedded 5-μm mid-LV sections were stained with hematoxylin and eosin (H&E); and for echocardiography and physiological measurements, terminal assessments were performed after 4 weeks under controlled heart rate (~350 bpm) and blinded conditions.', 'GSEA_configuration_description': "Gene Set Enrichment Analysis (GSEA) was performed using the fgsea R package. Proteins and phosphopeptides were ranked by differential expression (HFpEF vs. Sham) and analyzed in 'rank mode'. Gene sets were downloaded from the Bader Lab (Mouse_GOBP_AllPathways_no_GO_iea_October_01_2018_symbol.gmt). Enrichment results were visualized using the Enrichment Map app (Version 3.1) in Cytoscape (Version 3.6.1), with pathway themes grouped and labeled by AutoAnnotate (Version 1.2). Statistical significance thresholds were set to enrichment P < 0.01 and false discovery rate (FDR) ≤ 0.1. Overlap between gene set annotations was calculated using a combination of Jaccard and overlap coefficients with a cutoff of 0.375.", 'process_procedure_description': 'Proteomic and phosphoproteomic data processing and analysis were conducted using MaxQuant (version 1.3.0.5) with standard settings: search against the Swiss-Prot mouse database (downloaded January 24, 2019), allowing up to two missed trypsin cleavages; carbamidomethylation of cysteine as fixed modification; and variable modifications including oxidation of methionine, protein N-terminal acetylation, and phosphorylation of serine/threonine/tyrosine (STY) residues; precursor ion tolerances of 20 ppm (first search) and 4.5 ppm (main search); MS/MS peak tolerance of 20 ppm; and a stringent 1% false discovery rate (FDR) threshold for peptide, protein, and phosphosite identifications. Intensity-based quantification data were filtered (requiring quantification in ≥70% of samples), normalized using LOESS normalization via the LIMMA R package, and clustered using Omics Notebook (version not specified, cited as reference 45). Differential expression analysis employed a moderated t-test implemented in R (R Foundation for Statistical Computing). Statistical testing for histology, titin isoforms, and SIRT3 immunoblotting used GraphPad Prism software (GraphPad Software Inc., La Jolla, CA, United States) with two-tailed Student’s t-test (for normally distributed data) or Mann–Whitney U test (for non-normal distributions); significance defined as P ≤ 0.05.'}
{'sample_description': 'The study used skeletal muscle tissue samples obtained from the vastus lateralis of 18 healthy male participants (aged 20–40 years) via percutaneous needle biopsy under local anaesthetic (1% xylocaine) using a 5 mm Bergstrom needle with manual suction. Biopsies were collected at three time points: immediately prior to immobilization (baseline), and at day 3 and day 14 of unilateral left-knee immobilization (60° flexion, enforced via X-Act ROM Knee Brace). Each biopsy sample was washed with ice-cold saline, freed from visible connective tissue, and immediately snap-frozen in liquid nitrogen. A total of 54 muscle samples (18 participants × 3 time points) were processed for proteomic analysis.

Differential expression analysis identified differentially expressed proteins (DEPs) based on fold change (>2 or <0.5) and Student's t-test p-value (<0.05). Missing value imputation employed K-nearest neighbor (KNN) in R (version 4.1.2); standard batch correction and normalization were also performed in R. Principal component analysis (PCA) was conducted in R for visualization of inter-sample variation. Bioinformatics analyses—including GO (biological process, cellular component, molecular function) and KEGG pathway enrichment—were executed using the 'clusterProfiler' package in R (version 4.1.2), while the 'DEP' package was used for initial proteomics data processing. Protein-protein interaction (PPI) network construction utilized the STRING database (https://string-db.org) with DEPs as input, applying a combined interaction score threshold of >0.4 to define statistically significant interactions."}
{'sample_description': 'The study used human hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC) tissue samples from two clinical cohorts: a training cohort of 180 HCC specimens collected from January 2013 to December 2015 at Xiangya Hospital, Central South University; and a validation cohort of 120 HCC specimens collected from January 2012 to December 2015 at Hunan Cancer Hospital. Additionally, 50 matched fresh HCC tissues and adjacent nontumoral liver tissues (ANLTs) were collected from Xiangya Hospital from June to September 2020. All diagnoses were confirmed by two independent histopathologists. Cell lines included MHCC97-L, MHCC97-H, HCCLM3 (from Liver Cancer Institute, Fudan University), PLC/PRF/5, Hep3B, HepG2 (from ATCC), SMMC7721, Huh7, and primary human hepatocytes (PHHs) (from Chinese Academy of Sciences Cell Bank). All cell lines were cultured in DMEM supplemented with 10% FBS, 100 μg/ml streptomycin, and 100 U/ml penicillin at 37°C and 5% CO₂.', 'GSEA_configuration_description': "Gene Set Enrichment Analysis (GSEA) was performed using the Cancer Genome Atlas (TCGA) Liver Hepatocellular Carcinoma (LIHC) dataset to compare Sema3d high-expression versus low-expression patient groups. The analysis assessed enrichment of predefined gene sets related to signaling pathways, with specific focus on the PI3K/AKT pathway. Although exact software version, permutation type (e.g., phenotype or gene-set), number of permutations, metric for ranking genes (e.g., signal-to-noise ratio), and significance thresholds (e.g., FDR < 0.25 or nominal p < 0.05) are not explicitly stated in the provided text, the method states that GSEA was conducted 'between the Sema3d high- and low-expression patients in TCGA dataset' and that results were visualized and interpreted in conjunction with KEGG pathway analysis and supplementary tables (Supplementary Tables S5, S6).", 'process_procedure_description': 'RNA sequencing (RNA-seq) was performed to compare transcriptomic profiles between HCCLM3 control (HCCLM3 Ctr) and HCCLM3 cells stably overexpressing Sema3d (HCCLM3 Sema3d), identifying differentially expressed mRNAs (≥2-fold change; 75 upregulated, 282 downregulated). Subsequent bioinformatic analyses included KEGG pathway enrichment analysis and Gene Set Enrichment Analysis (GSEA) using the TCGA-LIHC dataset. GSEA was applied to stratify patients into Sema3d-high and Sema3d-low expression groups based on tumor mRNA levels, followed by assessment of pathway-level enrichment (particularly PI3K/AKT signaling) using standard GSEA methodology. Downstream validation involved Western blotting to assess protein expression and phosphorylation status of PI3K, AKT, and p-AKT in Sema3d-manipulated HCC cell lines, and Spearman’s rank correlation analysis to evaluate associations between Sema3d and p-AKT protein levels in 40 HCC tissue samples via immunohistochemistry.'}
{'sample_description': "The study included 13 squamous cell carcinoma patients (7 in the CAT+T group and 6 in the CAT+P group) enrolled in a double-blinded, randomized clinical trial comparing weekly intramuscular injections of 100 mg testosterone enanthate versus sterile saline placebo over 7 weeks, administered alongside standard-of-care chemo/radiation therapy.

(3) Differential expression analysis of metabolism-related genes was conducted with LIMMA in R, using p < 0.05 as the significance threshold. (4) Functional enrichment analyses employed DAVID 6.8 (for GO and KEGG), FunRich v3.1.3 (for cellular component, molecular function, biological process, and pathway validation), and GSEA 4.1.0 (as described above). (5) Protein–protein interaction (PPI) network construction used STRING database (string-db.org), followed by hub gene identification via cytoHubba plugin in Cytoscape 3.7.2. (6) Gene–gene interaction networks were built using GeneMANIA (genemania.org). (7) Hierarchical clustering of hub genes used ClustVis (biit.cs.ut.ee/clustvis/) with average linkage and Pearson correlation for distance metric. (8) SVM classifier construction used the e1071 R package with recursive feature elimination (RFE) and 10-fold cross-validation; ROC analysis and AUC calculation were performed in MedCalc v14.10.20. (9) Immune cell infiltration estimation used CIBERSORT with 1,000 permutations and p < 0.05 as the deconvolution success threshold. (10) Metabolomics data analysis included UHPLC-Q Exactive HFX Orbitrap MS acquisition controlled by Xcalibur software, PCA in SIMCA14, Volcano plots, chord diagrams, heatmaps, and bubble plots generated using TBtools (v1.099, Chen et al., 2020). (11) Integrated functional annotation and pathway analysis used Metascape (metascape.org/gp/index.html). (12) All statistical analyses (t-tests, Pearson correlations, hierarchical clustering) were implemented in R v4.0.5 and SPSS 17.0, with correlation strength interpreted using predefined r-value ranges (e.g., r = 0.60–0.79: strong)."}
{'sample_description': 'The sample used in the research for Gene Set Enrichment Analysis (GSEA) was the TCGA cohort of hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC), where UBE2T expression levels were dichotomized into high- or low-UBE2T phenotypes based on expression data; the analysis leveraged clinical and transcriptomic data from this publicly available, human patient-derived HCC cohort.', 'GSEA_configuration_description': 'GSEA was performed using the Molecular Signatures Database (MSigDB) C2 reference gene set (c2.cp.kegg.v7.0.symbols.gmt), representing curated KEGG pathways; UBE2T expression was used as the phenotype label to rank genes; gene set permutations were conducted 1000 times; significance was determined at a nominal p-value < 0.05; no additional software-specific parameters (e.g., weighted enrichment statistic, correlation metric, or false discovery rate cutoff) were explicitly stated beyond these settings.', 'process_procedure_description': "GSEA analysis was carried out using the standard GSEA software (Broad Institute); the input expression dataset was derived from the TCGA HCC cohort; gene ranking was based on signal-to-noise ratio or similar metric correlating with UBE2T expression (as implied by 'UBE2T expression was annotated as a high- or low-UBE2T phenotype'); the enrichment score was computed using the default weighted enrichment statistic; leading-edge analysis was applied to identify core enriched genes; results were visualized using GSEA’s built-in plotting tools to generate enrichment plots (e.g., Fig. 3A and Fig. 5C); all analyses followed standard GSEA protocol as implemented in version not specified, but consistent with widely adopted practices in the field."}
{'sample_description': 'The study used 35 pairs of cancer and paracarcinoma tissues from patients with colorectal cancer (CRC) collected between January and December 2014 at the Gastrointestinal Surgery Department of the Second Affiliated Hospital of Soochow University (Jiangsu Province, China); all specimens were stored at −80\u202f°C. Additionally, human CRC cell lines RKO, HCT116, SW480, and LoVo were purchased from GeneChem (Shanghai, China) and cultured in Dulbecco’s modified Eagle’s medium (DMEM) containing 10% fetal bovine serum (FBS) at 37\u202f°C with 5% CO₂. Lentiviral transduction was performed using shRNA lentivirus targeting DDX10 (sequence: 5′-GATGTGAGCAAGTTACCTATA-3′) and empty vector controls in HCT116 and RKO cells.

Pathway enrichment analysis was performed using the Kyoto Encyclopedia of Genes and Genomes (KEGG, http://www.genome.jp/kegg) and MetaboAnalyst (http://www.metaboanalyst.ca/), with significance determined by adjusted P-value (FDR < 0.05), metabolite count, and enrichment ratio. For 16S rRNA sequencing analysis, QIIME package (Version 1.9.1, http://qiime.org/install/index.html) was used for quality filtering, denoising, and OTU picking; UPARSE (version 7.1) was employed for OTU clustering at 97% sequence similarity; chimeric sequences were removed; taxonomic classification was performed using the RDP classifier Bayesian algorithm against the Silva 16S rRNA database with 70% confidence threshold; beta diversity and community composition analyses were conducted using QIIME and associated visualization tools. Statistical analyses (Student’s t-tests, Wilcoxon rank-sum tests, Fisher’s exact test, Spearman’s rank correlation) were performed in SPSS v22.0.'}
{'sample_description': 'The study used serum samples from 15 diagnosed lean MAFLD patients (lean-MAFLD group) and 15 healthy controls (Normal group), collected retrospectively from January to June 2023 at Nanjing Jinling Hospital. Lean-MAFLD patients were defined as having normal BMI (18.5–23.9 kg/m²), serum triglycerides ≥1.70 mmol/L, hepatic steatosis confirmed by abdominal ultrasound, and comorbidities including diabetes or arterial hypertension for at least 5 years, with failure of standardized medical treatment in the past 6–12 months. Exclusion criteria included other chronic liver diseases (e.g., viral hepatitis, autoimmune liver disease, malignancy), use of hepatotoxic drugs, or hazardous alcohol intake. Liver biopsy was performed on consenting participants to assess lobular inflammation and fibrosis; histopathological diagnosis was conducted by experienced pathologists using the Scheuer scoring system (inflammation: G0–G4; fibrosis: S0–S4; steatosis: F0–F3). Fasting serum (5 mL peripheral venous blood per participant) was collected, centrifuged, and stored at −80°C. All procedures were approved by the ethics committee of Nanjing University School of Medicine Affiliated Jinling Hospital and adhered to the Declaration of Helsinki, with written informed consent obtained from all participants.', 'HMDB_configuration_description': 'The HMDB database (http://www.hmdb.ca/) was used for functional annotation and enrichment analysis of differentially regulated bile acids (BAs). The method section does not specify any software configuration parameters, settings, or user-defined options for HMDB; it only states that HMDB was employed alongside the KEGG database for pathway annotation and enrichment analysis. No computational tools, interfaces, API calls, version numbers, filtering thresholds (e.g., p-value cutoffs, confidence scores), or integration protocols with HMDB are described in the text.', 'process_procedure_description': 'Data analysis was performed using multiple software tools and statistical procedures: (1) Unsupervised PCA was conducted using the prcomp function in R (www.r-project.org), with unit variance scaling applied prior to analysis; (2) OPLS-DA modeling, score plots, permutation plots, and VIP value extraction were generated using the R package MetaboAnalystR; data were log-transformed and mean-centered before OPLS-DA, and a permutation test with 200 permutations was performed to prevent overfitting; (3) Quantification of bile acid metabolites was carried out using Multiquant 3.0.3 software (Sciex); (4) ROC analysis for evaluating diagnostic power of differential BAs was performed using Sangerbox3.0 (http://www.sangerbox.com/tool); (5) Heatmap visualization of normalized differential BA metabolites (using Unit Variance Scaling) was generated with the R software package; (6) KEGG (http://www.kegg.jp/kegg/compound/) and HMDB (http://www.hmdb.ca/) databases were used for functional annotation and pathway enrichment analysis, though no specific parameters or settings for these database queries were reported.'}
{'sample_description': 'The samples consisted of tomato fruits collected at the breaker stage (Br) from SlSAHH2 over-expressed transgenic lines (OE-5# and OE-6#) and wild-type (WT) plants.

All bioinformatic analyses—including QC evaluation (one QC per 5–15 samples), multivariate statistics, and pathway mapping—were executed on the Majorbio cloud platform (https://cloud.majorbio.com).'}
{'sample_description': 'The study used two isolates of Streptococcus mutans: wild-type S. mutans UA159 (UA) and fluoride-resistant S. mutans UA-FR (FR), the latter selected after gradual adaptation to increasing sodium-fluoride (NaF) concentrations (from 50 µg/mL to 1,000 µg/mL) on BHI agar under anaerobic conditions (37°C, 95% N2, 5% CO2). Bacterial cultures were grown anaerobically in brain heart infusion broth (BHIB) supplemented with 1% glucose to either early log phase or stationary phase. Metabolite extraction was performed by rapidly quenching 5 mL broth with 10 mL of 60% methanol aqueous solution at −48°C, followed by centrifugation at 4500 r/min for 10 min at −19°C to discard supernatants; bacterial pellets were washed twice, then resuspended in 500 µL ice-cold 100% methanol (−48°C), subjected to three freeze-thaw cycles (liquid nitrogen/ice), and centrifuged at 16,000 × g for 5 min at −19°C to collect first supernatants; the pellet was re-extracted identically to obtain second supernatants, which were pooled with the first and stored at −80°C for LC-MS metabolomic analysis.', 'HMDB_configuration_description': 'Metabolite identification was performed by matching processed LC-MS peak features against the Human Metabolome Database (HMDB) version 2.0 (http://www.hmdb.ca); no specific software parameters, search tolerances, mass accuracy thresholds, retention time windows, or scoring criteria for HMDB matching are explicitly described in the text — only the database version and URL are provided.', 'process_procedure_description': "Raw LC-MS data were processed using peak extraction, peak matching, peak discrimination, peak alignment, total peak area normalization, data scaling, and missing value imputation via the '80% rule' (i.e., removal of features missing in >20% of samples within a group). Normalized peak area data were then matched against HMDB v2.0 and KEGG (http://www.kegg.jp) for metabolite annotation. Open-source MetaboAnalyst 6.0 (https://www.metaboanalyst.ca) was employed for metabolic pathway analysis. Orthogonal signal correction (OSC) was applied to filter group-independent variation, and multivariate statistical modeling was conducted using SIMCA-P 12.0 (Umetrics): principal component analysis (PCA) assessed sample aggregation, dispersion, and outliers; partial least-squares discriminant analysis (PLS-DA) identified major sources of inter-group variation; discriminant metabolites were selected based on variable influence on projection (VIP > 2.0) and statistical significance (Mann–Whitney test, p < 0.05); model validity was evaluated using R² (goodness-of-fit) and Q² (predictive ability) metrics derived from seven-fold cross-validation in SIMCA-P, where six groups trained the model and one group was predicted iteratively to determine optimal components and prevent overfitting."}
{'sample_description': 'Two sets of molecules were used: Set I (2,723 water-solvated molecules) and Set II (8,990 chloroform-solvated molecules), both sourced from HMDB 4.0. All compounds were non-salt forms containing only C, H, O, N, P, and S atoms, with molecular weights between 27 and 500 Da. Set I includes diverse chemical classes (e.g., organic acids, nucleosides, nucleotides, organoheterocyclic compounds, carboxylic acids, organooxygen compounds, benzenoids) as classified by ClassyFire; Set II covers organic compounds, organic acids, lipids, benzenoids, and organoheterocyclic compounds. Total nuclei counts: Set I has 29,489 carbon and 45,426 hydrogen nuclei; Set II has 138,535 carbon and 191,327 hydrogen nuclei. Molecular geometries and SMILES representations are provided in Additional file 1.', 'HMDB_configuration_description': '', 'process_procedure_description': "The study employed the In Silico Chemical Library Engine (ISiCLE) v1.0+ (github.com/pnnl/isicle), a Snakemake-based automated pipeline for high-accuracy quantum chemical property calculation. Input: SMILES strings.

Finally, cross-database coverage analysis was performed by mapping the final CID list against 50 manually defined exposome-relevant depositors (e.g., DSSTox, KEGG, ChEBI, EPA Substance Registry) as summarized in Table 1."}
{'sample_description': 'The sample used in the research was the ethanolic extract of Cyperus rotundus rhizomes (EECR), prepared by cutting dry rhizomes into small pieces, extracting with 95% ethanol at a 1:10 (w/v) ratio via maceration for 12 h in the dark at room temperature, followed by three rounds of reflux extraction in a water bath at 80°C for 2 h each; supernatants were combined and concentrated by reduced pressure distillation at 40°C, then lyophilized and stored at −80°C. For mass spectrometry analysis, EECR was dissolved in methanol to a concentration of 80 mg/mL.', 'HMDB_configuration_description': 'HMDB (Human Metabolome Database, http://www.hmdb.ca/) was used as one of the reference databases for compound identification in the phytochemical analysis of EECR; metabolites were identified by comparing experimentally obtained MS/MS fragmentation patterns and accurate mass measurements (error ≤ 5 ppm) against HMDB entries, alongside METLIN and NCBI databases; no software-specific configuration parameters, computational settings, or interface details for HMDB usage (e.g., version, search algorithm, scoring threshold, or API integration) were described in the method section.', 'process_procedure_description': 'The process procedure for metabolite identification involved UPLC-Q-TOF-MS analysis using Waters Acquity UPLC coupled with Bruker Impact II™ Q-TOF mass spectrometer with ESI interface; chromatographic separation was performed on a XDB-C18 column (4.6 mm × 100 mm, 1.8 μm) at 35°C with mobile phase A (water + 0.1% formic acid) and B (acetonitrile + 0.1% formic acid) under gradient elution (0–1 min: 5% B; 1–60 min: 5–40% B; 60–75 min: 40–57% B; 75–87 min: 57–95% B; 87–87.5 min: 5% B; 87.5–95 min: 5% B); flow rate: 0.5 mL/min; injection volume: 5 μL; ionization mode: positive ESI; capillary voltage: 4500 V; nebulizer pressure: 2.0 bar; drying gas flow: 8.0 L/min; drying gas temperature: 200°C; mass scan range: m/z 50–1000; acquired MS/MS spectra were matched against HMDB, METLIN, and NCBI databases with mass error tolerance ≤ 5 ppm to tentatively assign 21 compounds.'}
{'sample_description': 'Anonymized body fluid samples (urine from GA and SCAD patients) were diluted to analyte concentrations of ~10⁻⁷ M to sufficiently dilute higher-concentration sample components; reference standards included glutaric acid, ethylmalonic acid, glucose 1-/6-phosphate, galactose 1-/6-phosphate, N-acetylgalactosamine, N-acetylmannosamine, lactose, maltose, 3,4-dihydroxyphenylacetic acid, 2,5-dihydroxyphenylacetic acid, N-acetylglucosamine, and N-acetyl-L-phenylalanine, all obtained from Sigma-Aldrich; reference solutions were prepared at 10⁻⁷–10⁻⁸ M in 50:50 acetonitrile:H₂O; electrospray ionization was performed in both positive mode (+ESI) and negative mode (−ESI); for IR-IS measurements, ions were generated via direct electrospray without prior chromatographic separation; patient urine samples were analyzed in +ESI and −ESI modes to detect m/z features corresponding to protonated (m/z 133), deprotonated (m/z 131), and CID fragments (e.g., m/z 115 and m/z 113 after H₂O loss) of glutaric acid and ethylmalonic acid.', 'HMDB_configuration_description': 'The Human Metabolome Database (HMDB, Wishart et al.

Final classification applied bin-specific thresholds to candidate scores. Accuracy was assessed using Sensitivity, Specificity, PPV, MCC, and F-Score. Statistical significance was tested via Single-Factor ANOVA in Microsoft Excel 2007."}
{'sample_description': 'The study used cumulus cells (CCs) obtained from 54 women aged ≤35 years undergoing in vitro fertilization (IVF) with intracytoplasmic sperm injection (ICSI) at the Human Reproduction Section of São Paulo Hospital. All patients had tubal factor and/or mild male factor infertility, normal ovarian reserve (serum FSH 3–9 μg/ml on cycle day 3), and no endometriosis, PCOS, or ovarian dysfunction. Sperm samples were excluded if concentration was <3 million/mL or strict morphology (Kruger classification) was >4%. The CCs were collected prior to fertilization—specifically, after ultrasound-guided transvaginal oocyte retrieval and before ICSI—only from oocytes with expanded cumulus complexes. Samples were stored at −20\u202f°C until lipid extraction. The cohort was divided into two groups: 23 CC samples from women who achieved pregnancy (age 30.61 ± 3.88 years) and 31 CC samples from women who did not achieve pregnancy in that treatment cycle (age 30.97 ± 3.48 years).', 'HMDB_configuration_description': 'The Human Metabolome Database (HMDB; http://www.hmdb.ca) was used to identify lipid subclasses corresponding to differentially expressed ions detected via OPLS-DA S-plot analysis. A mass tolerance of 0.1 Da was applied during HMDB searching. Only ions with mass error ≤50 ppm (calculated as [(observed mass − theoretical mass) / theoretical mass] × 10⁶) were considered valid identifications. Lipid annotations included chemical formula, adduct type (e.g., M+Na[1+], M+H[1+], M+K[1+]), and structural assignments (e.g., PS(18:3(9Z,12Z,15Z)/14:1(9Z)), PC(22:6/22:2)). Multiple isobaric or structurally plausible lipid species were reported per m/z when supported by HMDB matches within the specified mass accuracy.', 'process_procedure_description': 'Data analysis was performed using MarkerLynx 4.1 and MarkerLynx XS software (Waters, Manchester, UK). Mass spectra were preprocessed with the following parameters: mass tolerance = 0.5 Da, baseline noise = 50, intensity threshold = 1,000 counts, and deisotope enabled. Principal component analysis (PCA) and orthogonal partial least squares discriminant analysis (OPLS-DA) were applied to the preprocessed MALDI-TOF-MS data (m/z 700–1200 range) to differentiate lipid profiles between pregnant and non-pregnant groups. The OPLS-DA S-plot was used to extract ions with highest covariance and correlation for biomarker discovery. Identified ions were subsequently matched against HMDB using 0.1 Da mass tolerance and ≤50 ppm mass error filtering. Statistical significance of group separation was evaluated via PCA score plots (PC1 vs PC2) and OPLS-DA 3D score plots (CP1 vs CP2), with model validation implicitly supported by clear group clustering and biological interpretability of lipid subclass assignments.'}
{'sample_description': 'The research utilized two human serum sample datasets (Dataset 1 and Dataset 2) acquired via ultra-performance liquid chromatography coupled with a hybrid quadrupole time-of-flight mass spectrometer (UPLC-QTOF MS). Raw LC-MS data were preprocessed using XCMS software. Dataset 1 consisted of 724 peaks annotated by CAMERA in positive ionization mode for adducts ([M+H]+, [M+Na]+, [M+K]+, [M+H−H2O]+), isotopes up to 4th order, and doubly charged ions, resulting in 497 annotation groups. Dataset 2 comprised 423 m/z peaks acquired in negative ionization mode without prior annotation, treated as monoisotopic deprotonated ions ([M−H]−). Both datasets were subjected to mass-based metabolite identification using MetaboSearch with a 10 ppm mass tolerance.

The software employs Java Web Start (JNLP) for cross-platform local execution (Windows/Linux/Mac) to reduce server load and bandwidth usage. Default parameters include 10 ppm mass tolerance, support for both positive and negative ionization modes, and dynamic real-time status synchronization between Java and Web engines.'}
{'sample_description': "The study used human glioblastoma cell lines LN229 and T98G, cultured in high-glucose Dulbecco's Modified Eagle Medium (DMEM) supplemented with 10% fetal bovine serum (FBS) and 1% penicillin/streptomycin, under humidified 5% CO₂ at 37°C. For metabolomics, LN229 cells (1 × 10⁷ per sample) were treated with corilagin at 0 µg/mL (Control group) and 256.8 µg/mL (Corilagin group) for 6 h (metabolomics sample preparation section) — though later experiments (e.g., Fig. 2 and Fig. 3) state 24 h treatment; six biological replicates were used per group. Cells were rapidly frozen in liquid nitrogen, homogenized, and extracted using 400 µL of L-2-chlorophenylalanine as internal standard, followed by vortexing (30 s), ultrasonication (40 kHz, 30 min at 5°C), protein precipitation at −20°C for 30 min, and centrifugation (13,000 g, 4°C, 15 min); supernatants were collected for LC-MS analysis. Quality control (QC) samples were prepared by pooling 20 µL from each sample supernatant.", 'KEGG_configuration_description': 'KEGG (Kyoto Encyclopedia of Genes and Genomes) was used for metabolic enrichment and pathway analysis to map differentially expressed metabolites. The study mapped each differential metabolite (identified via VIP > 1.0 and p < 0.05 from OPLS-DA and Student’s t-test) to pathways in the KEGG database. Pathway identification was performed based on metabolite–pathway associations in KEGG, followed by functional interpretation to identify significantly altered biochemical pathways. No specific KEGG version, API parameters, statistical correction method (e.g., hypergeometric test, FDR), or pathway significance thresholds (e.g., p-value or q-value cutoffs for pathway enrichment) beyond the metabolite-level selection criteria were explicitly stated in the text.', 'process_procedure_description': 'Metabolomic data processing and multivariate analysis were performed using Progenesis QI 2.3 (Nonlinear Dynamics, Waters, USA) for raw LC-MS data preprocessing, including peak detection, denoising, normalization, retention time alignment, and deconvolution, resulting in a peak list with mass-to-charge ratio (m/z) and retention time (RT). Multivariate statistical analyses were conducted using the ropls package (R package, version 1.6.2): principal component analysis (PCA) for unsupervised visualization of group distribution; partial least squares-discriminant analysis (PLS-DA) to assess model validity; and orthogonal partial least squares-discriminant analysis (OPLS-DA) to maximize separation between Control and Corilagin groups and identify metabolites driving group differences. Variable Importance in Projection (VIP) scores were calculated from the OPLS-DA model, and metabolites with VIP > 1.0 and p < 0.05 (two-tailed Student’s t-test) were selected as significantly altered. Fold change (Fc) was computed as the ratio of mean peak area in Corilagin group versus Control group. One-dimensional statistical analysis (Student’s t-test) was applied to assess significance of individual metabolite changes. Finally, metabolic enrichment and pathway analysis were performed using both KEGG and HMDB databases to annotate and functionally interpret the differential metabolites.'}
{'sample_description': 'The study utilized a traditional Chinese medicine formula named FSHJ, composed of six authenticated herbal materials: Baifupian, Cuguijia, Sharen, Ganjiang, Zhigancao, and Mahuang, in a dry-weight ratio of 6:4:3:3:2:2.

Statistical analysis used GraphPad Prism v8: data expressed as mean ± SD; comparisons performed via independent-sample t-tests or one-way ANOVA with Fisher’s LSD post-hoc test; significance threshold P < 0.05."}
{'sample_description': 'The sample used in the research is YHLS (a traditional Chinese medicine formula composed of six natural herbal medicines: JinYinHua, Shanyao, Digupi, Huangjing, Nvzhenzi, and Yiyiren, each at 9g, totaling 54g crude drugs), prepared by soaking in 270 mL pure water (solid-to-liquid ratio 1:5) for 30 minutes followed by three consecutive 30-minute aqueous decoctions; the combined extracts were concentrated to 38.5 mL yielding a high-dose solution (1.4 g/mL crude drug concentration), with medium- and low-dose solutions (0.7 g/mL and 0.35 g/mL) prepared by dilution; all YHLS samples were stored at 4°C until use. Additionally, biological samples included ear skin and blood from male C57BL/6 mice (8-week-old, 21.73 ± 0.98 g) induced with AD via topical MC903 (0.05 g/L, 20 µL/ear daily for 14 days) and treated with oral YHLS (3.5, 7, or 14 g/kg/day from day 8–14); control groups received vehicle only.', 'KEGG_configuration_description': "KEGG enrichment analysis was performed using the 'ClusterProfiler' R package with the screening criterion of P < 0.05; the analysis was conducted on the 102 overlapping targets identified between YHLS active ingredient targets and AD-related targets (retrieved from TCMSP and GeneCards); the top 16 most significantly enriched KEGG pathways (ranked by gene count and statistical significance) were selected for downstream KEGG-Target network construction; pathway-gene associations were extracted from the KEGG database to map target genes onto these pathways; visualization of enrichment results was performed using SRplot (https://www.bioinformatics.com.cn/srplot).", 'process_procedure_description': 'The process procedure involved multiple bioinformatic and experimental steps: (1) Network pharmacology analysis using TCMSP database (filtering by OB ≥ 30% and DL ≥ 0.18) and GeneCards for target retrieval; (2) Venn diagram generation for overlapping YHLS–AD targets; (3) Compound-target interaction network construction in Cytoscape 3.9.1; (4) PPI network construction via STRING database (Homo sapiens, confidence score ≥ 0.9, disconnected nodes excluded) and visualization/prioritization using Cytoscape 3.9.1 and CytoNCA plugin (calculating Betweenness Centrality, Closeness Centrality, Degree Centrality, Eigenvector Centrality, Local Average Connectivity, Network Centrality, and Information Content; two rounds of median-based filtering applied); (5) KEGG-Target network built from top 16 enriched KEGG pathways and associated targets, with core targets defined by highest degree values; (6) Molecular docking performed using CB Dock2 (online tool) after preparing protein structures (MAPK1: PDB ID 6G97; AKT1: PDB ID 4EJN) and ligand SDF files retrieved from PubChem; RMSD ≤ 2.00 Å was used as validation threshold for docking pose accuracy; (7) Experimental validation included UPLC-MS/MS (ACQUITY UPLC HSS T3 column, Thermo Orbitrap QE HF system), ELISA (JM-02339M1 kit), RT-qPCR (TransScript® All-in-One SuperMix, PerfectStart® Green qPCR SuperMix, ABI QuantStudio 1), Western blot (RIPA lysis, BCA assay, SDS-PAGE, PVDF transfer, primary antibodies from HUABIO/PTG, ECL detection, ImageJ quantification), H&E staining (commercial service), and statistical analysis using GraphPad Prism 10 (one-way and two-way ANOVA, P < 0.05 significance threshold).'}
{'sample_description': 'The study utilized human tissue samples from the GEO repository: liver tissues from normal controls and autoimmune hepatitis (AIH) patients (GSE159676), and brain tissues from normal controls and multiple sclerosis (MS) patients (GSE131279 and GSE131281). For in vivo validation, SPF-grade male C57BL/6 mice (18–20 g) were used.

(5) Data integration and visualization: Venn diagrams were generated to identify intersections between XYS compound targets and NRGs; all statistical analyses and figure generation (e.g., survival curves, heatmaps, UMAP plots) were performed using R with standard bioconductor packages."}
{'sample_description': 'The study utilized zebrafish (Danio rerio) as the primary biological model, including wildtype TüAB strain embryos for microinjection experiments, CRISPR/Cas9-generated washc3 knockout (KO) lines (washc3−/−), heterozygous (washc3+/−), and wildtype (washc3+/+) siblings, aged zebrafish (1-year-old) for histological and RNA scope analyses, and adult zebrafish hearts for proteomic, qRT-PCR, and Seahorse assays. Additionally, human ventricular cardiomyocyte AC16 cells were cultured and transduced with adeno-associated virus (AAV)-shRNA targeting WASHC3, and primary cardiomyocytes were isolated from adult zebrafish hearts. Embryos were collected at defined developmental stages: 48, 72, 96, and 120 hours post-fertilization (hpf) for phenotypic, functional, and molecular analyses; 72 hpf embryos were specifically used for Western blotting, birefringence, immunofluorescence, touch-evoked response, and cardiac function assays; adult heart tissue was harvested from 1-year-old zebrafish for proteomics and RNA extraction; and Tg(mnx1:mGFP) transgenic zebrafish embryos were employed for motor neuron morphology analysis.', 'KEGG_configuration_description': '', 'process_procedure_description': "No software tool named 'KEGG' or any KEGG-related bioinformatic analysis (e.g., KEGG pathway enrichment, KEGG Mapper, KAAS, or KEGG Orthology assignment) is described, mentioned, or implied in the provided Materials and Methods section. The computational and analytical tools explicitly cited include: ImageJ (version 1.53, NIH) for birefringence intensity quantification (mean gray value in skeletal muscle area); Tracker Video Analysis software (version 6.2.0, Open-Source Physics) for velocity and acceleration measurements in touch-evoked response assays; Proteome Discoverer 2.5 (Thermo Fisher Scientific) with Sequest HT search engine for LC-MS/MS data processing (fragment ion tolerance: 0.02 Da; parent ion tolerance: 5 ppm; enzyme: trypsin; variable modifications: Oxidation (M), Deamidation (N,Q), Acetylation (N-term), Met-loss (M), Met-loss+Acetyl (N-Term); fixed modification: Carbamidomethylation (C); precursor quantification: Top N Average, n=3); Perseus software (version 2.0.11, Max Planck Institute of Biochemistry) for proteomic data processing (log2 transformation, missing value imputation from normal distribution per replicate, ANOVA with p<0.05 threshold); GraphPad Prism 9 for statistical analysis (Mann–Whitney test, unpaired t-test, Kruskal–Wallis test, one-way ANOVA, two-tailed, p<0.05 significance level, SD or SEM representation). No KEGG database access, pathway mapping, enrichment analysis, or configuration parameters (e.g., organism ID, KO mapping, p-value cutoffs for KEGG terms) are reported."}
{'sample_description': 'Wild male Hilsa (Tenualosa ilisha) samples were collected from the Ganga River at Farakka, Murshidabad, West Bengal, India (24°48′1.34″ N; 87°55′21.53″ E) during August–November 2021 using a specially designed lift net. Fish were anaesthetized with MS-222 (120 mg/L water); no euthanasia was performed. A total of 18 male individuals (n = 3 per weight group) were selected across six distinct weight classes: H4 (501+ g), H22 (401–500 g), H37 (301–400 g), H35 (201–300 g), H42 (101–200 g), and H80 (50–100 g). Males were identified morphologically by abdominal shape and confirmed via dissection—characterized by thin, thread-like transparent gonads (testes), in contrast to broader, yellowish ovarian lobes in females.

SWATH-MS data normalization across samples involved removal of non-HCP entries (AAV capsid proteins YP_680426.1, Q9YIJ1, YP_077180.1, Q6JC40; Rep78 YP_680423.1; EGFP C5MKY7; ADH P00330; modified trypsin AC_000), followed by division of each HCP’s average normalized abundance (ng/μg) by the total average abundance of all detected HCPs in that sample, multiplied by 1,000 to yield ng/μg total HCP. Physical interaction networks for the top-100∩ HCP subgroup were mapped using StringApp (version 2.0.3) in Cytoscape (version 3.10.1) with a default confidence score cutoff of 0.4 for physical interactions. Functional enrichment (including Reactome) was executed using standard Reactome tools with FDR <0.05 as the significance threshold and Homo sapiens as the background genome.'}
{'sample_description': 'A total of 114 COVID-19 serum samples (200 µl each) were collected from 94 patients with single-organ morbidity (cardiovascular: 20, renal: 18, pulmonary: 20, gastrointestinal: 6, neurological: 13, hepatic: 17) and 20 controls (COVID-19 patients without organ morbidity). All samples were inactivated at 56 °C for 30 min and stored at −80 °C. For the discovery phase, 29 samples were used (cardiovascular: 4, renal: 4, pulmonary: 3, gastrointestinal: 6, neurological: 4, hepatic: 4, control: 4); for the validation phase, all 114 samples were used. Exclusion criteria included multiple organ co-morbidities, co-existing infections, or chronic ailments. Clinical phenotyping involved detailed history, physical examination, and documentation of pharmacological interventions.', 'Reactome_configuration_description': 'Reactome pathway enrichment analysis was performed as part of functional enrichment using the STRING database v12, with Homo sapiens as the background species. The analysis included Reactome pathways alongside KEGG and WikiPathways. Input proteins were differentially expressed proteins meeting criteria of 0.67 ≥ fold change ≥ 1.5 and statistical significance (FDR-adjusted p < 0.01). The Reactome analysis was conducted via the STRING web interface or integrated pipeline; no custom parameters or software-specific configuration settings (e.g., version, command-line flags, or internal thresholds beyond the stated FDR < 0.01) were reported for Reactome itself—only that results with FDR-adjusted p values < 0.01 were considered significant.', 'process_procedure_description': 'Bioinformatics analysis of differentially expressed proteins was performed using the STRING database v12 (Search Tool for the Retrieval of Interacting Genes/Proteins). Proteins satisfying 0.67 ≥ fold change ≥ 1.5 and statistical significance were submitted to STRING for functional enrichment. Enrichment analysis was run for three pathway databases: Kyoto Encyclopedia of Genes and Genomes (KEGG), WikiPathways, and Reactome. Homo sapiens was specified as the background species. Pathway results were filtered using an FDR-adjusted p-value threshold of < 0.01. No additional software-specific parameters (e.g., confidence score cutoff, interaction source weighting, or clustering algorithms) were explicitly described for STRING beyond the FDR filter and background species selection.'}
{'sample_description': 'The study utilized Epimedium sagittatum (Sieb. et Zucc.) Maxim., a Berberidaceae plant, purchased from Hunan Zirantang Traditional Chinese Medicine Pieces Co., Ltd. (production batch number: 230401) and authenticated by Professor Xiaohong Zhong. A standardized ethanol extract (EPE) was prepared by soaking 100 g of dried Epimedium in 8 volumes of 70% ethanol for 30 min, boiling for 1.5 h, centrifuging at 10,000 RPM for 30 min, collecting the supernatant, and repeating the extraction twice; supernatants were pooled and evaporated to dryness to yield a powder stored at −20°C. Six reference standards—hyperoside, epimedin A, epimedin B, epimedin C, icariin, and baohuoside I (purity ≥98%)—were obtained from Shanghai Yuanye Co., Ltd. The human pancreatic cancer cell line PANC-1 was acquired from Procell Co., Ltd.

Immunohistochemistry for periostin (Sigma HPA012306, 1:50 dilution) was performed on 3 µm FFPE sections after heat-induced antigen retrieval; slides were digitized using ScanScope® XT (Aperio) at ×40 magnification and quantified via Positive Pixel Count v9 (Aperio/Leica) software across three randomly selected tubulointerstitial regions (total average area = 209,290 µm²) per biopsy.'}
{'sample_description': 'The study used vocal fold tissue samples from six female New Zealand White rabbits (2.5 kg each) divided into two groups: hAEC-treated (n = 3) and saline-injected control (CTR, n = 3). Vocal fold injury was induced bilaterally via cupped forceps under pediatric endoscopic guidance, creating standardized defects extending to the superficial thyroarytenoid muscle. At the time of injury, 1 × 10⁶ human amniotic epithelial cells (hAECs) in 100 µL physiological saline were injected bilaterally into the center of the injured thyroarytenoid muscle in the treatment group; the control group received 100 µL saline only. After 6 weeks — corresponding to the early fibrotic ECM remodeling phase of scar maturation — rabbits were euthanized, and larynges were harvested. For proteomic analysis, the right vocal fold was excised, snap-frozen in liquid nitrogen, and stored at −80 °C until protein extraction. The left vocal fold was fixed in neutral buffered 10% formalin and paraffin-embedded for histology and immunohistochemistry. Additionally, one human vocal fold specimen from a deceased 14-year-old male (obtained postmortem with IRB-approved Decedent Research Request at Phoenix Children’s Hospital, fixed in formalin, processed at Mayo Clinic) was included for comparative myocilin immunofluorescent staining.', 'Reactome_configuration_description': 'Pathway overrepresentation (enrichment) analysis was performed using the Reactome Knowledgebase (www.reactome.com) with the following configuration: (1) Input consisted of differentially expressed proteins identified by mass spectrometry with non-adjusted p < 0.05 between hAEC-treated and CTR groups; (2) Species mapping was performed against the Homo sapiens Reactome pathways, with orthologous reactions available for Oryctolagus cuniculus (rabbit); (3) Statistical testing employed a binomial test to calculate enrichment probability for each pathway; (4) Multiple testing correction was applied using the Benjamini–Hochberg procedure to control the false discovery rate (FDR); (5) Pathways were ranked by ascending p-value and FDR; (6) Only pathways with FDR < 0.111 (as reported in Table 1) and containing at least one identified protein entity (e.g., COL1A2, LAMA3, EMILIN1) were considered enriched and reported.', 'process_procedure_description': "Proteomic data analysis and pathway enrichment were conducted using Reactome (www.reactome.com) as follows: First, differentially expressed proteins (with non-adjusted p < 0.05) identified via LC–MS/MS and Scaffold-based label-free quantification (NSAF) were compiled into a gene/protein list. This list was submitted to Reactome for overrepresentation analysis. Reactome mapped the input proteins to human pathways while accounting for rabbit orthologs where available. The binomial test assessed whether the number of input proteins mapping to a given pathway exceeded the expected count by chance, based on the total number of proteins in the Reactome human reference proteome. FDR correction was applied using the Benjamini–Hochberg method. Results were filtered to include only pathways with adjusted FDR ≤ 0.111 and containing ≥1 input protein (e.g., COL1A2, LAMA3, EMILIN1). Visualization and interpretation relied on the hierarchical Reactome pathway browser, with top-level categories (e.g., 'ECM biology') and sub-pathways (e.g., 'anchoring fibril formation', 'MET signaling', 'interleukin-4/13 signaling') reported with their respective p-values and FDRs. No custom parameters or software-specific settings beyond the default Reactome web interface configuration were described; all analyses were executed through the public Reactome website."}
{'sample_description': 'The study included 217 heart transplant recipients (76 with cardiac allograft vasculopathy [CAV] and 141 without CAV) recruited from the University Hospitals Leuven starting in 2014.

Results were filtered to include only pathways with adjusted FDR ≤ 0.111 and containing ≥1 input protein (e.g., COL1A2, LAMA3, EMILIN1). Visualization and interpretation relied on the hierarchical Reactome pathway browser, with top-level categories (e.g., 'ECM biology') and sub-pathways (e.g., 'anchoring fibril formation', 'MET signaling', 'interleukin-4/13 signaling') reported with their respective p-values and FDRs. No custom parameters or software-specific settings beyond the default Reactome web interface configuration were described; all analyses were executed through the public Reactome website."}
{'sample_description': 'The study included 217 heart transplant recipients (76 with cardiac allograft vasculopathy [CAV] and 141 without CAV) recruited from the University Hospitals Leuven starting in 2014. Urine samples were collected as 5 mL midstream specimens during routine follow-up visits. Inclusion criteria required CAV patients to have a confirmed diagnosis before or within 3 months of urine collection, while non-CAV patients required either a single negative coronary angiogram within 3 months of collection or two negative angiograms (before and after collection). Exclusion criteria included uncertain angiographic results (n=28) or failure to meet temporal diagnostic criteria (e.g., CAV diagnosed >3 months post-collection, n=3; non-CAV diagnosis outside the 3-month window without repeat angiography, n=57). Samples were bio-banked until analysis. The derivation cohort (n=108) and validation cohort (n=109) were formed via stratified random sampling to ensure equal distribution of CAV cases. All participants provided written informed consent, and the study was approved by the Ethics Committee of the University Hospitals Leuven (approval numbers B322201421186 and B322201421045).', 'Reactome_configuration_description': 'Enrichment pathway analysis of parental proteins corresponding to the 27 peptides in the final proteomic classifier was performed using the Online Reactome Pathway Database, version 75 (https://reactome.org). The analysis employed Bonferroni correction for multiple testing, and pathways were considered statistically significant if the corrected P value was < 0.05.', 'process_procedure_description': "The urinary proteomic classifier was developed using machine learning on peptide-level data derived from capillary electrophoresis coupled to mass spectrometry (CE-MS). After preprocessing—excluding peptides detectable in <90% of all participants or exhibiting high multicollinearity (Pearson correlation coefficient <0.7)—89 peptides remained for analysis. An extreme gradient boosting (XGBoost) model was implemented in Python 3.9 using the scikit-learn package (version 0.24.1) to discriminate CAV vs. non-CAV. The derivation cohort (n=108) was partitioned into 5 folds for cross-validation to assess internal performance and generalizability. Model hyperparameters were optimized to minimize overfitting via built-in regularization. The final model selected 27 peptides and was validated on an independent validation cohort (n=109). Diagnostic performance was evaluated using receiver operating characteristic (ROC) analysis, with the optimal probability threshold determined by maximizing Youden's index (sensitivity + specificity − 1) in the validation cohort. Additional statistical analyses—including univariate/multivariable logistic regression, Spearman correlation, integrated discrimination improvement (IDI), net reclassification improvement (NRI), and bootstrap-based 95% confidence intervals (1000 replicates)—were conducted in SAS 9.4."}
{'sample_description': 'The study utilized 160 high-throughput mRNA sequencing (mRNA-Seq) datasets acquired from the NCBI Gene Expression Omnibus (GEO), later reduced to 151 after quality- and balance-based filtering. These datasets originated from lymphoid (bronchial lymph node, retropharyngeal lymph node, nasopharyngeal lymph node, pharyngeal tonsil) and homogenized lung (healthy and diseased/lesion) tissues harvested from cattle during peak clinical signs of bovine respiratory disease (BRD). The cattle were experimentally challenged with isolated BRD pathogens including viruses (Bovine Respiratory Syncytial Virus [BRSV], Bovine Viral Diarrhea Virus [BVDV], Infectious Bovine Rhinotracheitis virus [IBR]) and bacteria (Mannheimia haemolytica, Mycoplasma bovis, Pasteurella multocida), or sham-challenged as controls.
